// The boss journal: Queen Skitterra, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
// Revamp pages: Fang Strike (the strand it leaves, with a knot to cut), Venom Spit (the green dot a hit leaves, cured only by mending to full)
// and Cocoon Drop (the husk that stays as cover for the Acid Deluge) were rewritten; Imprint, Cat's Cradle and Lure are new and Extreme only.
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).skitterra = {
  // the first time this hero meets her (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "Queen Skitterra: a spider the size of a barracks in a cave of pillars and silk, and the floor itself works for her. I kept her face on me and the line behind. My boots are glued to the flagstone and the silk did not let go of me till dawn. Will hold.",
    gloomsworn: "The Queen. A cave of pillars and silk, and a floor that works for her. I kept her face turned to me and the others behind. I have stood in worse dark, but never in anything so sticky. It clung to me the way the grave did.",
    thresher: "Mother Skitterra keeps her cave like a shuttered house: silk in every corner, eggs waiting in the walls, the floor gone soft and sticky. What a tidy hostess. Everything here comes due in its hour, and I mean to learn the hours.",
    bloodreaver: "The old mother! Legs everywhere, fangs like roasting spits, and a whole nursery in the walls. She bit me before I had finished grinning. A fine first scar. I am naming it after her.",
    runeguard: "The Broodmother keeps a cave of signs: lit legs, ripening eggs, threads drawn pillar to pillar. Every blow she deals is written somewhere first; I simply have not learned the hand. I filled the margins with guesses tonight. Most were wrong.",
    gorgemaw: "My hostess keeps a larder in silk: eggs, venom, guests wrapped for later. Everything bound and saved. After the ogre's careless kitchen I find this refreshing. I intend to eat through her pantry before she gets round to me.",
    oathbroker: "Her Majesty the Broodmother conducts her business through intermediaries: eggs, threads, venom. Every clause of her contract is hidden in silk. I have opened a second ledger beside the Ogre's, and begun reading what the silk hides.",
    ironpalm: "She speaks with many legs at once, a crowded sentence. I listened and understood little of it. The ogre never paused; this one never stops moving. Tomorrow I will find where one word ends and the next begins.",
    ranger: "Cave of pillars and silk. Bad sightlines, worse footing. It hunts the way I do, from the dark, and it is better at it. Respect. Need to learn where the pillars sit before I loose another arrow.",
    shadowblade: "My mark this time has eight eyes, which I found frankly excessive. Every one of them was on our knight. There is always a blind side. I found hers, and I still have the spoon.",
    pyromancer: "Her Leggy Majesty lives in a cave hung with silk, and silk, I am delighted to report, burns! She was less delighted. After the ogre, this felt like the real performance, and I want a second night.",
    thermist: "The spider. Cold count: pillars, silk, eggs, a queen. Then she moved and I ran hot all at once, everything in the walls ticking toward hatching. Cold-blooded, literally, and I am the opposite. I need to stay cold for this one.",
    driftblade: "The eight-legs lives in a cave strung with silk that catches you mid-stride, which is a crime against anyone who likes to run, and I ran anyway, round and through and out again, and she nearly had me twice.",
    marionettist: "Our leading lady makes a grand entrance: legs, fangs, and a nursery waiting in the wings. The set is all pillars and silk. A better class of villain than last week's, and she has her own strings. My Doll was terrified. (I was not. I was at the back.)",
    cleric: "The spider queen fights with poison, hunger and her own children. I counted every bite on our people tonight and prayed over each. This one will test my patience, and my supply of candles.",
    grovekeeper: "The old spinner keeps her cave like hard country, dear: eggs ripening in the walls, silk on every path, the ground itself uncertain. After the kitchen's weather, a quiet kind of cold. My lot came back. That's a start.",
    chronomancer: "Skitterra. I will have met her a great many times by the end of this, I suspect. Tonight she was a tangle of legs and timing, eight things happening slightly out of step, and I did not yet know the tune.",
    alembist: "Specimen: a broodmother spider, venomous, damp cave. Ailment profile: poisons, bites, eggs, sticky floors. A long shelf of remedies wanted. Note: silk is less flammable than I had hoped. Tested. Twice.",
    threadweaver: "Another seamstress, and a sloppy one: threads strung any old way, pillar to pillar, snagging anyone who walks through. My little stitches came home tangled. I will be unpicking her work for weeks.",
    tidecaller: "Pillars, silk and a great slow spider in the middle of it all: a tide pool at noon, still and close, with everything in it waiting for its hour. I stood well back from her face and listened for her rhythm. She has one.",
    lanternkeeper: "Pillars, silk and one great spider in the middle of the dark. I lit a lantern wherever the web was thin and found them all too far from where we fought. Tomorrow I light the fighting ground first and the walking ground after.",
    kenseier: "A spider the size of a hall, and she moves like a blade that has never needed to hurry. Her legs came down on a beat I had not learned yet, and her silk was in my boots by dawn. Tomorrow I count the Queen.",
    houndmaster: "Spider the size of a barn, and she hunts from the dark the way I do. Rust hates the webs and he was right. Pillars everywhere, and silk in the boots. Tomorrow we learn where the pillars stand. Down, Rust.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats her
  win: {
    bastion: "The Queen fell at the hub with the floor gone round us, and we finished her standing on her own silk. Every soldier accounted for, every boot still on a foot. I have never been so pleased to be stuck.",
    gloomsworn: "The Queen fell at the hub with the floor gone out from under us, and we finished her standing on her own silk. Everyone is alive. The watch is relieved, and I am not sorry to be.",
    thresher: "Mother Skitterra came in at the hub, on the last of the good ground. In time, and nicely done. I said a few words over her, as I do for anything I have attended, and swept the eggshell into a neat heap.",
    bloodreaver: "The old mother's down! Every bite she gave me, I gave back with interest. My arms are more scab than skin. The finest scars I own. I will miss her.",
    runeguard: "The Broodmother is dead, and every sign she wrote, we read in time at last. Her final fall I saw a full breath ahead. I have bound the margins together as a volume.",
    gorgemaw: "My hostess, served at last. Tough in the legs, bitter in the venom, generous in the portions. I have had shorter meals and few finer. I am keeping a fang to pick my teeth.",
    oathbroker: "Her Majesty has defaulted on every obligation, and we have collected in full. Her estate is a ruined cave and some eggshell. The second book is shut.",
    ironpalm: "She said everything she had to say, with every leg, and we answered every word. Then she was still. I counted my breaths until my hands stopped shaking, and bowed to the silk.",
    ranger: "Brought it down on the last strip of floor. Long hunt. Clean kill. Kept a fang for the quiver.",
    shadowblade: "The mark died at the heart of her own web, still glaring at our knight. Fitting. It never saw me, not once, not even at the end.",
    pyromancer: "She's down, the cave is in cinders, and I am a legend! The silk went up like a tapestry at a feast, and I stood in the glow of it all the way to the exit. Entirely magnificent.",
    thermist: "Down. Cold, clean, done. Then it all came rushing up hot and I laughed until my hands shook. I sat down on the last sound strip of web and stayed there.",
    driftblade: "We beat her and I ran one last lap of the cave through the torn silk for the joy of it, round every pillar left standing and out the far side, never landed once.",
    marionettist: "Curtain! Our leading lady takes her final bow at the hub and does not rise for an encore. My Doll is unharmed and insufferable about it. (Applause from the rafters.)",
    cleric: "The spider queen is gone, and every one of us walked out of the dark. I said a quiet prayer for her children too, and lit a candle for the mother.",
    grovekeeper: "The old spinner's season is over, dear. The cave went quiet like a field after harvest. My lot are tired and scratched and all still standing. A good year.",
    chronomancer: "Skitterra is dead. I would say at last, but from where I sit she always was going to be. We only had to find the right bar of the song.",
    alembist: "Specimen deceased. Remedies used: all of them. Shelves bare, robe scorched, one explosion, entirely intended. Note: her venom makes a lovely base for something. Bottled.",
    threadweaver: "The competition is undone. Every thread she strung, we cut or walked. My stitches held together through all of it. I kept one of her threads for the sampler. Pulled it tight, at last.",
    tidecaller: "She fell at the hub with the web gone from under us, and the cave went slack as water after the turn. Every one of us walked out on our own feet. I sang a while for the quiet.",
    lanternkeeper: "She fell at the hub and the web went slack beneath us. Four of us went in and four walked out, with the lanterns burned low but not out. I left a light on the pillar by the way home.",
    kenseier: "The Queen is down. Her blows came softly and on time, and I learned to answer each. I left the web as one leaves a practice floor, with thanks to the thing that kept me on it.",
    houndmaster: "Queen's down. Silk off the dogs' coats took the whole evening, and not a growl out of either. Both stood for it like lambs. Supper was late.",
  },
  moves: {
    'Venom Mist': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Her breath rolled green over the whole cave, and a shield is no use against weather. I drew the line in by the healer before it reached us, braced, and let it pass over.",
      gloomsworn: "Green fog, over all of us. I took the others in beside our healer ahead of it. Fog is only a quieter kind of dark, and it reaches everywhere the same. I breathed it, out of habit, and felt nothing.",
      thresher: "Her green breath rolls over the whole cave and nowhere escapes it, which is almost polite: everyone is treated alike. We stood close by the menders, everyone whole beforehand, and let it settle on all of us at once.",
      bloodreaver: "The whole cave choked on her breath! I barely felt it, which is shameful, but the soft ones did, so we bunched up by the menders, all of us whole beforehand, and drank it down together.",
      runeguard: "She swells, she exhales: the sign is plain and the sentence has no exceptions. Be whole beforehand and gathered within reach of mending, for the mist spares no corner. Not foreseen the first time. I have corrected the page.",
      gorgemaw: "A broth served to every seat at once, thin, bitter, faintly green. There was no refusing it, so we gathered by the healers, full beforehand, and swallowed together. Underseasoned. Not even a bay leaf.",
      oathbroker: "A levy upon the whole company in common, and recurring. It cannot be avoided, only met solvent. We gathered by our healers, whole beforehand, and paid it. Entered in the ledger, second book.",
      ironpalm: "She spoke to all of us at once, in one long breath. There was no corner to hear it from. We gathered by the menders and let it pass through.",
      ranger: "Mist filled the whole cave. No lane out of it, no high ground. Came in close to the menders before it rolled and took it standing. Eyes watering. Mine.",
      shadowblade: "Breathing on everyone at once is ill-mannered, even for a spider. Nowhere to slip away to, so I stood close to our menders and waited for the air to clear. Unpleasant, never dangerous.",
      pyromancer: "A green fog, everywhere at once, the colour of trouble! Nowhere to escape it, so I stood by the healers and cast straight through. The mist dulled my glow for a moment, which I suppose was a kindness to everyone else.",
      thermist: "Nowhere is safe, so be whole before it comes. Then the mist was on us, wet and hot in the throat, and I was already tucked in with the others. Steadier than I felt.",
      driftblade: "The mist rolled through the whole cave and there was nowhere to run, which I hated, so I ran in close to the menders instead and let it blow through all of us at once.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady breathes upon the entire house. No exits, no wings. The company gathered downstage by the healers and took it as one. (The Doll coughed, theatrically.)",
      cleric: "The mist reaches every one of us, every time. I made sure nobody was still hurting from the last thing before she breathed, and laid the light over them all at once.",
      grovekeeper: "Her breath comes on like fog off a marsh, dear, over every field at once. I planted on my lot while it was still gathering in her, so the mending was rooted when it touched them.",
      chronomancer: "She breathes on the whole cave, and later breathes again; it is the chorus of her song. I had everyone whole a beat before it, every time after the first. The first, we will not discuss.",
      alembist: "Observation: venom mist, whole party, no escape. Dose: everyone topped up beforehand, the antidote brewed in the quiet before her breath. Note: it does not keep; brew it fresh each time.",
      threadweaver: "The mist reached every stitch at once. I drew the whole hem in close to me, so the threads could carry the hurt between us evenly. A mist is a poor seamstress, but she is thorough.",
      tidecaller: "Her breath came green over the whole cave, and by now I knew its hour. I drew everyone in close and let the water climb until it was high when the mist arrived, so it broke on a full shore.",
      lanternkeeper: "Her breath rolled green across the whole cave, and no pillar could turn it. I kept the lanterns close and the party closer, and let them all burst as the mist arrived. Everyone was already mended when it reached them.",
      kenseier: "Her breath rolled over the whole cave, and a blade cannot turn a mist. We drew in beside the healer before it reached us and stood in it together.",
      houndmaster: "Her breath rolled over the whole cave. Nothing to dodge, so we crowded the healer and stood in it together, dogs pressed to my legs. Mended before it came. Sit tight.",
    } },
    'Silk Wrap': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She swung the wrap at me with her forelegs, and the sweep in front of her reaches wide. Shield up in the last instant, the line kept clear of her front. It pinned my feet after. Bad footing, at last meant literally.",
      gloomsworn: "She swung her forelegs at me, wide in front of her. I took it with the dark drawn tight and kept the others clear of her face. It pinned my feet after. I do not mind being held still.",
      thresher: "The wrap comes due on our knight alone, a wide sweep off her front legs, then his feet stuck fast like a post in clay. Poor dear, but it is his hour. I worked her flank, well off her face, humming, with nothing due for me.",
      bloodreaver: "The old mother wrapped me in silk and bit straight through it. Deep, and it stung! Then my feet stuck, so I made her come to me. The others kept to her sides, out of the sweep.",
      runeguard: "Her forelegs draw back toward whoever holds her. A ward the breath before; everyone else away from her front, for the sweep is broad. It binds the feet after, so choose your ground first. I did, which is the whole art.",
      gorgemaw: "She served the wrap straight to me, as a proper hostess should: silk outside, a bite within. I opened wide just before, then stood stuck to my chair, chewing. Overcooked.",
      oathbroker: "The wrap is addressed to me as principal: a heavy blow, with a clause binding my feet afterward. I arranged my defences before signing, and the others kept clear of her front.",
      ironpalm: "She wrapped me in silk, then bit. I raised my guard in its last instant. My feet would not move after, so I answered from where I stood.",
      ranger: "Front sweep went for the knight. Wide arc. Kept to her flank, well outside it. Knight stood planted after.",
      shadowblade: "The mark flung its silk in our knight's face, as it always does. I was behind it, naturally, where the wrap never reaches.",
      pyromancer: "She lashed silk at our knight, wide as a fan in front of her! I had planted myself behind her already, so I kept right on casting. The knight came out glued to the floor, glowing with indignation.",
      thermist: "The knight takes the wrap; the rest of us stay off her face. It hit so hard the silk hissed, and he stood stuck while she moved on. I kept to her side, cold, counting the knight's breaths.",
      driftblade: "She flung the wrap at our knight out front, and I went round through her side and out the back, well clear of the sweep. He stood stuck to the floor like a heron, poor thing.",
      marionettist: "She wraps the knight in silk, front and centre, and the company keeps to the wings at her sides. The knight is rooted to his mark, a fine piece of blocking.",
      cleric: "The wrap came for our brave knight; it lands heavy, then holds him in place. I laid the light on him before it struck, not after, and the rest of us kept away from her face.",
      grovekeeper: "The wrap falls on our shield like hail on a single field. I sowed on him before it came, kept my lot off her front, and he stood rooted after like a fencepost.",
      chronomancer: "The wrap comes for the one in front, then holds his feet a while. It lands on the off-beat before her legs begin striking, so he must already stand where the first strikes won't fall. He will have; he did.",
      alembist: "Wrap and bite on our knight, heavy, with a binding after. Mending applied before it lands, everyone else off her front. Note: the binding wears off on its own; no reagent needed. I tried one anyway.",
      threadweaver: "The wrap is meant for our knight alone, so I kept every other stitch out of her front and ran a thread to him to share the bite.",
      tidecaller: "The heavy wrap was meant for the knight alone, who held her front while the rest of us kept to her flanks. I watched her legs rise and had the water at its crest on him as the silk fell. It pinned his feet after.",
      lanternkeeper: "The heavy wrap was for our knight alone, who held her front while the rest of us kept to her flanks. I hung a lantern on him as her legs rose, so the glow was on him when the silk fell, and left it there while he was held fast.",
      kenseier: "Her forelegs swept at me, wide across her front. I met them with the blade as they landed, and the others stayed off her face. A late press leaves the silk on my feet, which is a lesson in itself.",
      houndmaster: "Forelegs swept wide at the shield-man. Kept to her flank, well outside the arc, and the dogs with me. Stay off her face and she can't reach you.",
    } },
    'Fang Strike': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "One fang down one lane, drawn from her to my boots. I stepped out sideways at the last instant, and the line kept clear of the strip behind me. The silk it left across the floor slows whoever wades through it. A blow or two at its knot cuts it loose.",
      gloomsworn: "A fang down one lit line, from her to me. I took a step out of it, and the others stayed out of the strip behind. It left a strand of silk with a knot at the far end. Wading it is slow going, so I broke the knot instead.",
      thresher: "The fang comes down one lane at our knight, drawn before it falls. He steps off it sideways, and the rest of us stay out of the strip behind him. It leaves a silk strand with a knot at the end, dear. A tap or two cuts it, or just walk round.",
      bloodreaver: "She lanced a fang straight down at me! I took the first one for the scar, then learned the sidestep, and anyone lined up behind me ate the rest. The silk she leaves drags at your feet. One or two hits on the knot at its end and it's gone.",
      runeguard: "One lane, written from her fangs to whoever holds her. Step off it sideways, and keep the party out of the strip behind. The thread it leaves is a footnote with a knot at its tip: break the knot, or walk round the thread. It slows anyone who wades it.",
      gorgemaw: "She skewered one lane down the table at me, mouth to plate. I stepped out of it sideways, and nobody sat in the strip behind me. It left a strand of silk across the floor, sticky as noodles. One or two bites at the knot on its end and it parts.",
      oathbroker: "A single lane, drawn from her fangs to the principal; notice is given before it is served. I stepped clear sideways, and the others kept out of the strip behind me. The silk it leaves is a standing lien, slowing any who wade it. The knot at its end discharges it.",
      ironpalm: "A fang down one line, her to me, drawn first. I stepped aside in the last breath, and the others stayed out of the strip behind. The silk it left lay across the floor and slowed whoever stepped in it. A blow or two to its knot, and it fell.",
      ranger: "Fang down one line, her to the knight, drawn from the start. Knight steps off it. I stay out of the strip behind him. Strand left on the floor, knot at the far end. Wading it is slow. A couple of arrows into the knot and it drops.",
      shadowblade: "She stabbed one lane at our knight, drawn on the floor before the stroke. He stepped out, and I was never in the strip behind him. She left a strand of silk, rudely, with a knot at its far end. I cut it on my way past.",
      pyromancer: "The lane at our knight again! He hopped out sideways, and I stood well off to his side, still casting. The silk it leaves slows you like honey, so I burned the knot at its end with a single bolt. Two, if I was being showy.",
      thermist: "The lane at the knight, drawn cold and early. He stepped out, and I kept off the strip behind him. Then the silk, a strand that dragged at my feet like cooling wax. I ran hot to the knot at its end, a hit or two, and it fell slack.",
      driftblade: "A fang came down one lane at the knight and I, already running, kept clear of the strip behind him. It left a strand of silk that slows anyone running through it, which is unforgivable, so I crossed the knot at its end, twice, and the strand fell slack.",
      marionettist: "A single lane, lit from her fangs to the knight before the strike. He steps out sideways, and the company keeps out of the strip behind him. She leaves a silk trip-wire across the stage with a knot at its end. Strike the knot and it falls. (The Doll took a bow.)",
      cleric: "A single lane, from her fangs to our knight, and he stepped out of it sideways at the last moment. I asked the others to keep out of the strip behind him. The silk it left slowed anyone who waded through it, so someone kindly cut the knot at its end.",
      grovekeeper: "She drove a fang down one furrow at our shield, and he stepped out of the row. Keep your lot out of the strip behind him, dear. What she leaves is a strand of silk like bindweed across the ground, slow going. A knock or two at the knot and it withers.",
      chronomancer: "A fang down one lane at our knight, drawn ahead of the stroke. He steps out as it lands, and none of us stood behind him. The strand it leaves slows whoever wades it, and a hit or two at its knot undoes it. I cut one already.",
      alembist: "Single lane, fang to shield, marked on the floor first. Step off sideways; the strip behind the knight is struck as well. Residue: a silk strand across the floor, slowing whoever crosses it. Fix: a hit or two at the knot on its end. Note: I tested the slowing once.",
      threadweaver: "She ran one taut thread of a fang from her mouth to the knight, shown before it was pulled. He slipped sideways out of the seam, and I kept my stitches clear of the cloth behind him. Her strand stays lying there, so I unpicked the knot at its end.",
      tidecaller: "A fang came down one lane at our knight, straight as a current. He stepped aside, and the rest of us stayed out of the strip behind him. It left a silk strand that dragged at the feet like weed. A blow or two at its knot let it go.",
      lanternkeeper: "A fang came down one lane at the knight, drawn before it fell. He stepped out sideways, and I kept my light off the strip behind him. It left a silk strand with a knot at its far end, so I set a lantern by the knot and we cut it.",
      kenseier: "One thrust, one line, and the line was shown before the thrust. Our knight stepped off it on the beat before it fell, and I kept my guard off the strip behind him. The silk it left slows anyone who wades it. A stroke or two at its knot cuts it.",
      houndmaster: "A fang down one lane at the shield-man, drawn on the floor first. He stepped out sideways, and the dogs and I stayed out of the strip behind him. Left a silk strand across the floor, knot at the end. Rust runs through everything, so I cut it. A hit or two.",
    } },
    'Eightfold Lunge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Her legs lit up in order, and she struck each way and its opposite at once, turning as she went. I stepped onto the ground the last strike had just left and followed it round. Ground just struck is spent.",
      gloomsworn: "Her legs flared one by one, and each flaring leg stabbed forward and back as she turned. I waited for the last blow, then moved onto the spot it had just left, and went round her with the turn.",
      thresher: "Her legs light up in order, end to end, and each strike goes both ways at once while she turns. I stepped onto the ground just struck and followed the turn round her. A struck place is not struck twice, so it is a tidy dance.",
      bloodreaver: "She stabbed both ways at once, turned, and stabbed again! Took one the first night. Memorable. Now I watch the lit legs and step in behind each strike as it passes.",
      runeguard: "Her legs light in sequence, and the sequence is the order of the strikes: a table of contents. Each strike takes a direction and its opposite; step into the ground just struck and trail the turning.",
      gorgemaw: "This course comes in pairs, from opposite ends of the table, turning like a lazy carousel. The trick is to sit where the last plate was just cleared. Nothing sent back.",
      oathbroker: "The terms are written on her legs, which light in the order she will strike, each blow paired with its opposite. The relevant clause: ground just struck is paid off. I followed the debt round.",
      ironpalm: "Her legs spoke in order, two words at once, back to back, turning. I stepped into the place she had just spoken. Then the next.",
      ranger: "Legs light in order. Read them like tracks. It strikes both ways, turns, strikes again. Moved in behind each strike, onto ground it had finished with.",
      shadowblade: "In front and behind at once, then a turn, then again. So I stepped into the ground she had just struck; she never looks back at what she has already hit. Rather sweet of her.",
      pyromancer: "Her legs lit up in order like a chandelier, and then the chandelier started stabbing. I hopped into each spot she had just struck, right behind the turn, casting all the way.",
      thermist: "Lit legs, the order, the turn. Each strike hits a way and its opposite. Step where it just landed. The ground hissed at my heels as I chased the turning round her. Close. Right.",
      driftblade: "She stabbed both ways, turned, stabbed again, and I followed each strike into the ground it had just left, a leaf in a whirlpool, round and round her.",
      marionettist: "A turning dance, striking upstage and downstage at once. Her lit legs are the cue sheet. The company stepped in behind each strike. (My Doll pirouetted.)",
      cleric: "Her legs light before she strikes, both directions at once, turning each time. I told our young archer twice to step in behind the last strike, not ahead of the next. Nobody stumbled.",
      grovekeeper: "Her legs light in order before she strikes, like a wind veering round the compass. Ground just struck is quiet ground, dear. I kept stepping into it, behind the turn, and my lot came with me.",
      chronomancer: "A round in strikes: both ways at once, a turn, again. The ground she has just struck is safe for the rest of the verse. I stepped in behind each beat, which I will have done before, surely.",
      alembist: "Strikes in opposed pairs, rotating. Read the lit legs, then step into whichever ground was struck last. Note: anyone standing ahead of the turn spoils quickly. I keep a list of names.",
      threadweaver: "She lunges both ways at once and turns between strikes. I walked my stitches in behind each one, onto ground it had just finished with, nobody straying ahead.",
      tidecaller: "Her legs lit in turn, a strike each way and then the turn of her. Ground just struck is slack water, so after each blow I walked onto the place it had left and went round with her, never ahead.",
      lanternkeeper: "Her legs lit one after another, a strike each way and then a turn of her whole body. I stayed behind the last blow and never ahead of the next, carrying a light round the spider as she turned.",
      kenseier: "Her legs lit one by one, and each stabbed forward and back as she turned. I counted the strokes, waited for the last, then stepped onto the ground it had left and went round with her. The beat was in the order.",
      houndmaster: "Her legs lit up in turn, and each stabbed ahead and behind as she wheeled. Counted the lights, waited till the last one, then went onto the ground she'd left. Dogs behind me.",
    } },
    'Silk Lines': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She strung lines between pairs of us. A long line wanted its ends far apart; on the hard nights a short one wanted its pair side by side. Each pair kept its ground until the silk let go. Orders are orders, even from a spider.",
      gloomsworn: "Silk joined us in pairs. A long one meant walking apart until it drew straight; a short one, on the harder nights, meant standing shoulder to shoulder. We stayed put till it let go. I know something about being tied to another.",
      thresher: "She strung silk between me and the archer, a long line, so we walked to opposite ends of the cave till it was taut as a bowstring and waited it out. A short one wants the pair shoulder to shoulder instead.",
      bloodreaver: "Silk between a pair of the others! The long one they pulled taut, far apart as the cave allows. The short ones stayed close as lovers. No scars for anyone. Disappointing, honestly.",
      runeguard: "A thread drawn between a pair of the party. A long thread is stretched until it sings, a short one kept close until it fades. Its length tells which; the Broodmother is nothing if not consistent.",
      gorgemaw: "Silk strung between diners, like noodles. The long ones you stretch until they are thin as thread; the short ones you eat side by side. Al dente, when handled properly.",
      oathbroker: "A bond binding a pair of the company. A long thread: stretched to its full term. A short one: kept close until discharged. The clause that matters is the length.",
      ironpalm: "Silk between me and the mage. Long. We walked apart until it was taut, and waited. Short threads ask for closeness instead. I listened for which.",
      ranger: "Got a line to the mage. Long one. Walked to opposite sides of the cave till it pulled taut. Short ones stay close. Easy, if you read which.",
      shadowblade: "She tied me to our poor alchemist with a long thread. We drifted to opposite walls until it drew tight, and it was left holding nothing. Short ones, I am told, prefer company.",
      pyromancer: "Tied by silk to the archer! A long line, so we stalked off to opposite ends of the cave, very dramatic. Short lines, I learned the hard way, want you cosy instead.",
      thermist: "A long line, far apart; a short line, together. Hold it until it lets go. I nearly ran back in too early, rushing, hot. Too far is right, for once.",
      driftblade: "She tied me to the archer with a long thread and for once I got to run far and fast, clear across the cave until it hummed, and then wait there, still, which I hated.",
      marionettist: "A pair of the company are tied with silk. A long line: exits at opposite wings. A short line: a duet, close together, centre stage. (Hold the pose until the line fades.)",
      cleric: "She ties our people together with silk. A long thread wants them far apart; on harder nights a short one wants a pair nearly arm in arm. I kept close watch until each one faded.",
      grovekeeper: "Lines of silk between my lot, like runner beans on a string, dear. A long one wants pulling taut, far apart; a short one wants tending close. Each pair kept to their own row.",
      chronomancer: "Silk between a pair of us, for a slow count. A long thread: far apart until it lets go. A short one: close until it lets go. Next time, which was last time, nobody guessed.",
      alembist: "Silk between a pair of us. Remedy, by label: long line, far apart; short line, close together; hold the dose until it fades. Note: do not mix up the labels. I once labelled both long.",
      threadweaver: "Her lines, between my stitches. Long ones want tension: far apart, pulled tight. Short ones want no slack at all: side by side. At last, a seamstress who knows what she wants.",
      tidecaller: "Silk joined us in pairs, and the length told what it wanted: a long line wanted its two far apart until it drew straight, a short one, on the hard nights, wanted them shoulder to shoulder. Each pair stayed put until it let go.",
      lanternkeeper: "Silk tied us into pairs, and the length said what it wanted: a long line, far apart until it ran straight; a short one, on the hard nights, shoulder to shoulder. We kept still until it let go. I lit each pair's patch of floor on its own.",
      kenseier: "Silk tied us in pairs, and the length said what to do: a long thread meant stand far apart, and on the hard nights a short one meant stand close. Then it was a matter of staying put until it parted. I have waited longer for less.",
      houndmaster: "Silk tied us two by two. Long thread, stand far apart; on the hard nights a short one, stand close. Then stay put till it parts. Told the dogs to stay too. Rust doesn't.",
    } },
    'Spin': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Threads strung pillar to pillar round the middle. Crossing one stings and pins the feet, so we walked round and broke the knots at the pillars to cut them for good. I logged the knots left to right. Neat footing, afterwards.",
      gloomsworn: "Threads ran from pillar to pillar about the middle. To cross one is to be stung and pinned, so we kept round them and broke the knots at the pillars, and the threads stayed cut.",
      thresher: "She strung threads pillar to pillar round the middle, a web nobody asked for. Walk through one and it stings and holds you. I broke the knots by the pillars instead; every thread cut now is one less coming due in the dark. Tidy.",
      bloodreaver: "Silk across the cave, pillar to pillar. Walked through one: a sting, then stuck. Barely a scratch, shameful. The knots by the pillars break, and a broken knot drops its thread. So I broke them.",
      runeguard: "Silk drawn pillar to pillar, a square about the hub. Never cross a thread; break its knot beside the pillar and it is cut for good. Each one left whole she will use later, in the dark. I numbered mine in the margin.",
      gorgemaw: "Silk across the dining room, pillar to pillar, and stinging. Each strand is tied off by a knot at its pillar; I bit through the knots and the strands fell. Chewy. No flavour at all.",
      oathbroker: "She has drawn threads pillar to pillar, which sting and detain any trespasser. Each is secured by a knot beside its pillar; break the knot and the thread is void. Every thread she keeps is a claim on us later.",
      ironpalm: "Silk between the pillars. Walk through it, and it stings and holds. I did not walk through it. I broke its knot by the pillar instead.",
      ranger: "Threads between the pillars. Square round the middle. Step through one and it stings and roots. Shot the knots at the pillar ends; each one drops a thread.",
      shadowblade: "Silk hung across the room like a burglar's bell. Step through and it stings and holds you. Each thread has a knot by its pillar; I slipped round the outside and opened them one by one.",
      pyromancer: "Silk strung pillar to pillar! I walked through one in a moment of artistic passion, and it stung and held me. Each thread is tied off with a knot by its pillar, and knots burn. Gloriously.",
      thermist: "Threads in a square, pillar to pillar; don't cross; the knots by the pillars cut them. I crossed one in a rush and it stung and stuck me. Went back for the knot, and cooled.",
      driftblade: "She strung threads right across my lanes, which is unforgivable, and walking through one stings and sticks you, so I broke the knots by the pillars and then ran through where they had been.",
      marionettist: "The set is strung with silk, pillar to pillar, and the company must not walk through it; it stings and holds. Each strand is tied off at a pillar. (Strike the set: knots first.)",
      cleric: "Threads between the pillars sting and hold whoever walks through; our impatient rogue learned that twice. Break a knot by its pillar and that thread is gone. I asked them to, kindly, and they did.",
      grovekeeper: "She strung silk round the middle like a fence round a pasture, dear. Walking through it stings and holds you. The knots by the pillars are its posts; pull those up and the fence comes down.",
      chronomancer: "Threads, pillar to pillar; they sting and hold whoever walks through. Their knots break, and every thread cut now is one fewer from her in the dark, later. Later, which I have seen.",
      alembist: "Threads pillar to pillar; crossing one stings and binds. Break the knot by its pillar and the thread is cut for good. Note: each thread left whole is a spider dropping on us in the dark.",
      threadweaver: "A sloppy seam, pillar to pillar, snagging anyone who walks through. Each thread ends in a knot by its pillar; unpick the knot and the whole thread falls. I set my stitches to unpicking.",
      tidecaller: "Threads ran from pillar to pillar about the middle, and anyone who walked through was stung and held. I went round. The others broke the knot at each pillar's foot, and the thread fell with it. A knot is only a held breath.",
      lanternkeeper: "Threads ran between the pillars around the middle, and anyone who walked through was stung and held. I went round. The others broke the knot at each pillar's foot, which dropped that thread, and I kept a lantern beside the knots for them.",
      kenseier: "Threads ran from pillar to pillar round the middle, and crossing one stings and pins. We kept round them and broke the knots at the pillars, so the cuts stayed cut. I learned the knots in order, left to right, like a form.",
      houndmaster: "Threads strung between the pillars, and crossing one stings and pins you. Kept round them and cut the knots at the pillar ends. Held the dogs back, they'd have run straight through.",
    } },
    'Venom Spit': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She spat a narrow line at the two farthest from her. They stepped aside as it came and kept to the edge, so the pool lay by the wall. Whoever it struck wore a green dot over the head and bled on until our healer mended them to full.",
      gloomsworn: "She spat a narrow stream at the two farthest off. They sidestepped as it flew and kept to the edge. One was struck anyway, and a green dot sat over his head and drew him down slowly. Only being mended to full ended it. I know something about slow poison.",
      thresher: "She spits a thin green line at the two standing farthest out. They step aside late and leave the puddle by the wall. If it lands, a green dot sits over the head and keeps collecting, dear, until the healer has you whole again.",
      bloodreaver: "She spits at the two who keep their distance, never me. They sidestep at the last and the puddle lands by the wall. One I saw caught: a green dot over his head, bleeding on and on, till the healer filled him back to the brim. Not a scar worth having.",
      runeguard: "She turns toward the two standing farthest, and they sidestep the narrow line as it comes. The pool lies at the edge, out of everyone's way. One struck carries a green dot over the head and bleeds slowly; the sentence ends only when the healer has restored them entirely.",
      gorgemaw: "Spittle sent to the two guests seated farthest away. They stepped aside at the last, and the sour pool gathered by the wall. One took it full, and a green dot sat over her head, simmering, until the healers had topped her up to the brim. A slow reduction.",
      oathbroker: "A charge levied on the two who keep their distance. They stepped aside at the last and left the residue at the wall. Whoever it struck carries a green dot over the head, accruing slowly, and it is discharged only when the healers restore them in full.",
      ironpalm: "She spoke to the two furthest away. A thin, sharp word. They stepped aside as it came, and it pooled by the wall. One was struck anyway, and a green dot hung over her head and bled her slowly. It stopped when the healer made her whole.",
      ranger: "Spits at the two farthest. Often me. Narrow line. Stepped sideways at the last, left the pool at the wall. Took it once: green dot over the head, slow bleed. It stays until the healer tops you off, so I stood within reach of the mending.",
      shadowblade: "Spitting at the two farthest has never been aimed at me; I am at her back. Our archer sidestepped it and left the puddle by the wall. Whoever was struck wore a little green dot over the head until the healer made them whole. Poison is so impolite.",
      pyromancer: "She spat at me for standing at a sensible distance! I stepped aside just as it came and left the pool glowing at the wall. One of us took it, though, and a green dot sat over her head and nibbled away until the healer filled her up to the top.",
      thermist: "Two farthest, a narrow line. Step aside late; keep the puddle at the wall. I dodged too soon, it followed, and I had to jump again. Late is right. One struck wore a green dot over the head, a slow scald, until the healer topped them to full.",
      driftblade: "She spat at me out at the edge, thin and green, and I slid sideways at the last moment, through the gap, and the venom pooled behind me at the wall. One of us caught it and wore a green dot over the head until the healer filled her up again.",
      marionettist: "The back row is spat upon: the two farthest from the stage. They step aside at the last moment and leave the puddle in the wings. Whoever is hit wears a small green dot over the head and bleeds on until the healer restores them in full. (The Doll took no part.)",
      cleric: "She spits at the two standing farthest away. They stepped aside as it came and kept the pool by the wall. If it lands, a green dot sits over the head and wears them down slowly, and it does not stop until I have mended them to full. I mended quickly.",
      grovekeeper: "She spits at the two far out, a thin green line, and leaves a bog behind. They step aside late and keep it by the wall. A hit leaves a green dot over the head, like blight on a leaf, creeping on until I have mended the sapling to full.",
      chronomancer: "A thin line at the two farthest. The aim settles an instant before it lands; step aside on that, not sooner. The puddle stays by the wall. A hit leaves a green dot over the head, ticking slowly, until the healer has them whole. I will have mended sooner.",
      alembist: "Venom spat at the two farthest, leaving a slowing pool. Stand far, step aside late, keep the pool by the wall. Anyone struck wears a green dot and a slow drain, and the only cure is mending to full. A partial dose leaves it working.",
      threadweaver: "She spits at the two stitches farthest out. They stepped aside late and kept the puddle by the wall, off our hem. One caught it anyway, and a green dot sat over their head, fraying them slowly. I mended them whole, and the fraying stopped.",
      tidecaller: "She spat a thin green line at the two farthest out, and the poison pooled where it fell. They stepped aside late, so it lay against the wall. One was struck, and a green dot sat over their head and drew them down slowly, until the water rose to full.",
      lanternkeeper: "She spat at the two who stood farthest from her. They stepped off at the last breath, so the pools ended by the wall. One was struck, and a green dot hung over their head, burning slowly down. I kept a light on them until they were whole.",
      kenseier: "A thin line of venom went at the two farthest from her. They stepped aside as it flew, so it pooled by the wall, off our floor. One was struck: a green dot over the head, and a slow bleed. It ends only once the healer has them whole.",
      houndmaster: "She spits at the two furthest. That was me, once. Stepped aside at the last and the pool stayed by the wall. One lad took it full, green dot over his head, bleeding slow. Healer had to fill him to the top before it let go. Dogs stayed near the middle.",
    } },
    'Egg Sacs': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Egg sacs fell round the web, each wanting one of us on it. One soldier to each sac, feet planted; the goo holds you a moment after. Leave one empty and it bursts on everyone. Hard nights only, and I counted them like rations.",
      gloomsworn: "Sacs fell across the web, and each needed a body trampling it. One was mine to stand on. Standing in it sticks you a while. If one is left whole, it bursts over us all. A body is a good plug; I have been used for that before.",
      thresher: "Egg sacs dropped round the web like fallen lanterns, and each wants one of us treading it flat. I took my own and stood stuck a moment in the goo, quite happily. Leave one lying and it bursts over the whole cave. Hard nights only.",
      bloodreaver: "Egg sacs! One of us on each, stomp it flat. It splashes and grips your feet, a sticky little sting. Leave one alone and it bursts over everyone, and that's not even a good scar.",
      runeguard: "Sacs settle on the web, each within its own ring. One of us to each, standing inside; the stomp holds the feet a moment. An unattended sac bursts upon us all and fouls its ground. Count the rings, then the bodies.",
      gorgemaw: "Caviar! Sacs scattered on the web, one portion each. We each stepped onto our own; mine was chewy and clung to my boots. Leave one uneaten and it splatters every plate.",
      oathbroker: "On the hard nights she lays sacs, each demanding the signature of exactly one of the party. One per sac, feet held a moment after. An unsigned sac defaults upon us all.",
      ironpalm: "Sacs on the web. Each asked for one of us. I answered mine with my foot. It held me a moment.",
      ranger: "Sacs on the web, apart from each other. One of us each. Took the near one, stood in it, stomped. Feet stuck a moment. None left whole.",
      shadowblade: "Eggs, laid in the open. Careless of the mark. One of us to each sac; I took the one behind her, obviously. The stomp held my feet a moment. Worth it.",
      pyromancer: "Egg sacs dropped round the web, one for each of us! I stomped mine flat, mid-spell, and got stuck in the goo. Leave one and it bursts over everyone, which would ruin the whole evening.",
      thermist: "Sacs on the web; one each; step in; stomp. The goo held my feet and she was already winding up behind me. Leave none. Cool and quick.",
      driftblade: "Egg sacs dropped and each wanted one of us standing still on it, the worst thing she could ask of me, so I ran to mine, stomped it, stuck a moment, and ran on.",
      marionettist: "Egg sacs appear, each with a spotlight for a single performer. One of the company to each mark; leave one empty and the whole house is splashed. (My Doll took a mark. Bravely.)",
      cleric: "On harder nights she lays sacs across the web. Each wants one of us standing on it, and whoever does is held a moment. Leave one, and it bursts over us all. Blessed are the feet that stay put.",
      grovekeeper: "She sows egg sacs round the web like seed. Each wants one of us to tread it in; leave one and it bursts over the whole field and sours the ground. My lot took one apiece.",
      chronomancer: "Sacs, one to each of us. Step on yours before it bursts. Your feet stay put a moment after the stomp, so do not plan on moving straight away. I will have learned that, I am sure.",
      alembist: "Egg sacs, each needing one body. One of us per sac, stomp, accept a brief binding. Note: an untended sac bursts over everyone and leaves a pool. Spoiled stock.",
      threadweaver: "Egg sacs on the web, each wanting one stitch standing on it. I sent one to each, no doubling up, none left bare, like pins in a cushion. They held fast a moment after stomping.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights she laid sacs across the web, each wanting a body to trample it. I took the nearest. The goo held my feet a long breath after, and a sac left whole bursts over every one of us.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights she laid sacs across the web, and each needed someone to walk onto it. Whoever did was held for a moment, so I hung a lantern on them. A sac left whole bursts over all of us. One light for each, none left unclaimed.",
      kenseier: "Sacs fell round the web, each wanting one body on it. I took one and planted my feet, and the goo held me a while after. A sac left bare bursts on all of us, so none was left bare.",
      houndmaster: "Sacs on the web, one body to each. Took one, planted my feet, stomped it. Goo holds you a while after. A bare sac bursts on everybody, so none went bare.",
    } },
    'Strand Reel': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She shot a strand to a far pillar and hauled herself down it. Anyone on that line is hit and stuck, so we stepped off it. I marched to her pillar, and when her legs lit there the strikes swept across the cave from the wall. Read them from where she hangs.",
      gloomsworn: "She loosed a strand at a far pillar and dragged herself along it. Anyone in that path was struck and held, so we stepped out. I went to the pillar first, and her next strikes came from there, sweeping in from the wall. Her legs still showed the order.",
      thresher: "She throws a strand to a far pillar and draws herself down it like a bead on a string. I stepped off the line, then strolled over to her pillar. If her legs light there, the strikes come across the cave from the wall, and I count them from where she sits.",
      bloodreaver: "She threw a strand at a pillar and flew down it! I was in the way once. It stung. Now I step off the line and run after her, and when she strikes from that pillar the blows sweep in from the wall, so I read her legs and slip in behind each one.",
      runeguard: "A strand flung from her to a distant pillar: the line is the sentence. Leave it before she reels herself along it, then follow. If the legs light there, the strikes are written from the wall inward, and the order is the same. I stood on the line once, as a test.",
      gorgemaw: "She slurped herself along a strand to a far pillar like a noodle, flattening anyone in the way. Step aside, then follow her to her new seat. Her next course is served from that seat, sweeping in from the wall. Rather elegant, for a flattening.",
      oathbroker: "She files a strand to a distant pillar and travels upon it, collecting from anyone on the line. Step off before it is executed, then attend her at her new address; any strikes that follow are served from there, across the cave from the wall.",
      ironpalm: "She threw a line to a pillar and went along it. I was not on the line. Then I walked to her, and when her legs lit I watched them. Her blows came from the wall, across the room, in the same order.",
      ranger: "Strand from it to a far pillar. It reels itself along. Stepped out of that lane. It hung on the pillar after, and its next strikes came from there, sweeping across the cave. Same order of legs. I held the long side.",
      shadowblade: "A strand to a pillar and she flung herself down it. Anyone on the line was hit and held; I had already stepped aside. Then I followed, and when she struck from her pillar the blows swept in from the wall. Her legs still gave the order.",
      pyromancer: "She shot a strand to a pillar and zipped along it like a comet, flattening anyone in the way! I stepped out of its path. She hung there after, and her next strikes swept in from that wall, so the close fighters chased her and read her legs.",
      thermist: "A strand to a far pillar; get off the line. She shot down it right past me, and I ran after her, quick. She waits at the pillar, and her next strikes come from there, across the cave. Go to her, and read her legs from there.",
      driftblade: "She flung a strand to a pillar and flew along it. I slipped off the line, then chased her through, round the pillar, and found her hanging there. Her next strikes came across the cave from that wall, and I ran with them.",
      marionettist: "She flies on a wire to a far pillar, and anyone beneath the wire is struck and held. The company clears the path, then the close fighters follow her across the stage. Her next strikes are played from that wing, sweeping in from the wall.",
      cleric: "She casts a strand to a far pillar and pulls herself along it, striking and holding anyone in the way. Our people stepped off the line, and those who fight close went after her. Her next strikes came from there, across the cave. I followed with the lantern.",
      grovekeeper: "She cast a strand to a far pillar and hauled herself down it like a river in spate. Stand clear of the channel, dear. She rests at the pillar after, and her next blows came in from that wall, so the close fighters went to her and read her legs.",
      chronomancer: "A strand to a pillar, then she follows it. Leave the line before the pull. Once she arrives she lingers a beat, and her next strikes come from there, across the cave. The near fighters go to her. Every time.",
      alembist: "She reels herself to a far pillar, striking and binding anyone on the line. Clear the line, then those who fight close follow her over. Note: whatever she strikes next, she strikes from there, sweeping in from the wall. Go while the pillar is warm.",
      threadweaver: "A strand from her to a pillar, then she reels herself down it, catching any stitch left on the line. I kept mine off it, and the close ones followed her across. Her next strikes are stitched from that wall, across the cave, in the same order.",
      tidecaller: "She shot a line to a far pillar and hauled herself down it like a rip, catching whatever lay in the channel. We stood out of that lane. She rested at the pillar after, and her next strikes rolled in from that wall. We walked over and read her legs.",
      lanternkeeper: "She shot a line to a far pillar and hauled herself along it, striking whatever lay in the lane. We kept out of the lane. She rested at the pillar after, and her next strikes swept in from that wall. I carried my lantern over to the close fighters.",
      kenseier: "She shot a strand to a far pillar and hauled herself down it. Anyone on the line was struck and stuck, so we stepped off it. I walked to her pillar ahead of her. Her next strikes came from that wall, and I answered them in order.",
      houndmaster: "She shot a strand to a far pillar and hauled herself along it. Stepped off the line, and the dogs too. Anyone on the road gets knocked flat and stuck. Her next strikes came from that pillar, across the cave from the wall.",
    } },
    'Fang Rush': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights she charges whoever stands farthest. Her line follows them until just before. They stepped aside late, or put a pillar in her way. Then nobody walks the silk she drags.",
      gloomsworn: "On the harder nights she runs down the farthest of us, the path trailing them to the last. A late sidestep, or a pillar between, and she misses. Then nobody walks her silk.",
      thresher: "On the hard nights she runs at whoever stands farthest, her line following them like a hound after a hare until just before. Step aside then and not a moment sooner, or put a pillar in her way. Then keep off the sticky track she leaves, dear.",
      bloodreaver: "She runs down the farthest one herself! Not me, sadly. They wait until the last moment and step out of her line, or hide behind a pillar and let the stone take the fangs.",
      runeguard: "She fixes on whoever stands farthest, and her line follows them until a breath before. Step aside late, or set a pillar in her way; then avoid the sticky trail she leaves. The timing is a matter of nerve, not scholarship. I practised the nerve.",
      gorgemaw: "Whoever sat farthest from the table, she rushed. They stepped aside late, or sat behind a pillar, and she got a mouthful of stone. Her trail stays sticky, like spilt syrup.",
      oathbroker: "A charge against whoever sits farthest. Her line follows the debtor until just before execution; step aside then, or interpose a pillar as surety. Her trail is a lien. Avoid it.",
      ironpalm: "She ran at the farthest. Her line followed them until just before. They stepped aside then. Her trail was sticky; I walked round it.",
      ranger: "Charges the farthest. Usually me. Line tracks you to the last moment. Put a pillar between us; she hit stone. Don't walk her trail after.",
      shadowblade: "She charges whoever stands farthest. Rude. Her line follows them, then settles; that is when you step aside. A pillar in the way works too. The trail she drags is sticky. Avoid it.",
      pyromancer: "She charged me, straight across the web, which I took as a compliment. I waited until her aim settled, then leapt aside, and she thundered past trailing sticky silk. Pride: wounded.",
      thermist: "Farthest one; wait for the aim to settle; step aside, or a pillar between. She went past so close I felt the wind of her, a scald of air. Then her trail, sticky. Keep off it.",
      driftblade: "She charged at me across the web and I waited, hating every still moment, until her line settled, then slipped aside and she ran straight through where I had been and left sticky silk behind.",
      marionettist: "She rushes the back row. The back row waits for her aim to settle, then exits. A pillar onstage serves as well. (She left sticky silk all over the set.)",
      cleric: "She runs down whoever stands farthest away, and her line follows them until just before. Our archer slipped behind a pillar and let the stone take it. Then we kept off the silk she left.",
      grovekeeper: "She charges the one farthest out, like a gale down a valley. Her line settles just before; step aside then, or put a pillar in its path. The silk trail stays sticky a while, dear.",
      chronomancer: "A charge at the farthest. The aim tracks them until the last tick before it lands; move on that tick, or let a pillar take it. Her trail lingers. I waited for the beat, as usual.",
      alembist: "A charge at the farthest. Step aside once the aim settles, or let a pillar absorb it. The pillar is chipped for its trouble, and the trail she leaves is sticky; spoiled boots are the usual complaint.",
      threadweaver: "She charges the stitch that has wandered farthest. They wait for her line to settle, then step aside or slip behind a pillar. Mind the sticky silk she drags behind her; it ruins a hem.",
      tidecaller: "She ran down whoever stood farthest, the path following them until the very last. They stepped aside late, or put a pillar between, and she met stone. I kept off the silk she dragged behind her.",
      lanternkeeper: "She ran down whoever stood farthest, her path following them until the last instant. They put a pillar between themselves and her, and she met stone. Then I kept every light well away from the silk she trailed behind.",
      kenseier: "She ran down whoever stood farthest, the line trailing them until late. A late step aside, or a pillar in her road, and she went by. Then nobody walked the silk she dragged.",
      houndmaster: "She ran down the furthest of us. A pillar in her road and she hit stone. Never walk the silk she drags behind. Called the hounds in so she couldn't pick them off.",
    } },
    'Brood': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The ripest eggs hatched, a spiderling on each side of the web. They must die together, or the survivor goes wild. We brought both low evenly and finished them in the same breath. Two posts, one order.",
      gloomsworn: "Eggs split on both sides of the web and a spiderling crept out of each. They live and die together; one left alive goes mad. We cut both down level and finished them at the same moment.",
      thresher: "The darkest clutches hatch a pair, one each side of the web, and they won't be parted. Cut one early and the other runs wild. We brought both down even and took them in together, a tidy pair of partners. Better still, break the eggs before they hatch.",
      bloodreaver: "A pair of little ones hatched across the web! Kill one and the other goes mad. So we bled them both down evenly and finished them together. Cute. Bite hard.",
      runeguard: "The ripest clutches swell, and hatch as a pair, and the pair must fall together, for one left alone turns savage. Better still, break the clutches before they ripen. A hatchling is a sentence not yet finished.",
      gorgemaw: "Her children came out in pairs, one at each end of the table. Eat one and the other takes offence. So we ate them together. Soft-shelled. Delicate.",
      oathbroker: "The ripest clutches hatch, an heir each, and their estates are joint. Settle one and the other defaults violently, so settle both at once.",
      ironpalm: "A pair hatched, across the web. When one is silenced, the other screams. So we silenced them together.",
      ranger: "Pair hatched on opposite sides. Split our shots, brought both down together. Kill one alone and the other goes wild. Fewer eggs before, fewer problems now.",
      shadowblade: "Her children hatched in a pair. Kill one and the other takes it personally. So we ended them together, which is tidier anyway.",
      pyromancer: "Eggs hatched! A pair of spiderlings, across the web from each other. Kill one and the other loses its temper. So we burned them down evenly, together. Also: break eggs early. Fewer spiders.",
      thermist: "A pair hatches, across the web; kill together. We killed one too fast and the other went mad. Fury. Level, then: wear them evenly, finish together.",
      driftblade: "A pair hatched across the web from each other and they hate dying alone, so we wore both down evenly, me running between them through the middle, and they died together.",
      marionettist: "A pair of spiderlings take the stage, one in each wing. Kill one and the other throws a tantrum, so the company finished them together. (Better still: break the eggs before their entrance.)",
      cleric: "Her children hatched as a pair, and they do not like dying alone; when one falls, the other rages. We brought both low and ended them together. I prayed for them a little.",
      grovekeeper: "The ripest clutches hatched in a pair, across the web. Reap them together, dear, or the one left standing runs wild. Better yet, break the clutches before they ripen.",
      chronomancer: "A pair hatches. Kill one, and the other has a few beats before it turns wild. So they fall on the same beat, or near it. The clutches can be broken beforehand, of course.",
      alembist: "A pair of spiderlings, hatched across the web. Wear both down evenly, kill together; one alone turns savage. Note: a clutch broken early hatches nothing. Cheapest remedy on the shelf.",
      threadweaver: "A pair hatched across the web, tied to each other like a double stitch. Cut one and the other snaps wild. So we wore them down evenly and cut them both at once.",
      tidecaller: "Her young hatched two at a time on opposite sides of the web, and neither will die alone; the one left standing goes wild. We eased both down level, as the sea levels two shores, and ended them in the same breath.",
      lanternkeeper: "Her young hatched two at a time, and the one left standing goes wild. We took each pair down together and level, and finished them in the same breath. Two young, two glows kept steady, and no wick burned to a stub.",
      kenseier: "The ripest eggs hatched, one spiderling to a side. They must die together, or the survivor goes wild. We cut both down level and finished them on the same beat. One count for two blades.",
      houndmaster: "Two spiderlings hatched, one each side. Kill one alone and the other goes wild, so both went down together. Sent a dog at each. Fewer eggs earlier, fewer fangs now.",
    } },
    'Imprint': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Acid pooled in three places, and the young latched onto whoever stood nearest, who wore an orange sign. Mine trailed only me. Nothing I swung would bite it until it had crossed acid, so I skirted a pool and it went through. Then it fell like any other.",
      gloomsworn: "Green pools lay across the floor. Each hatchling chose the nearest of us and kept to them alone, an orange sign hanging over that head. It could not be harmed on dry ground. I went round a pool, let it wade through, and cut it down. I have been followed by worse.",
      thresher: "Three acid pools, and each hatchling picked the nearest of us for a partner, an orange sign over their head. Nothing harms it on dry ground, dear. So I stepped round a pool, my partner took the short way through, and after that it could be cut. A tidy dance for two.",
      bloodreaver: "Acid pools on the floor, and a hatchling stuck to me like a leech, an orange sign over my head. Nothing hurt it until it had crossed acid. I walked round a pool, it followed straight through, then I tore into it. I stood in the acid once. Worth the sting.",
      runeguard: "Three pools of acid are laid across the floor, and each hatchling fixes upon whoever stands nearest, who then wears an orange sign. It follows that one alone; steel does nothing to it until the acid has touched it. Go round a pool, never through, and let yours cross. Then kill it.",
      gorgemaw: "Acid puddles on the floor, and each little one adopted the guest nearest, an orange sign over his head. Mine would not be chewed on dry ground. So I strolled round a puddle, it went straight through, and then it was swallowed. Sour.",
      oathbroker: "Acid pools across the floor, and each hatchling bound itself to whoever stood nearest, who carries an orange sign. It follows its holder alone and is immune until the acid has touched it. I walked round a pool, it crossed, and the matter was settled.",
      ironpalm: "Acid pooled on the floor. Each hatchling chose one of us and followed only them, an orange sign over that head. Mine would not feel my hands until it touched the acid. I stepped around the pool. It did not. Then it could be struck.",
      ranger: "Pools of acid, three. Hatchlings each took the nearest of us, orange sign overhead, and tailed that one only. On dry ground, nothing hurt them. Put a pool between us and let it cut straight through. Then it dropped to a few arrows.",
      shadowblade: "Three pools of acid, and each hatchling chose whoever stood nearest and clung to them, an orange sign over that one's head. Untouchable until it crossed the acid. I stepped round a pool and let mine blunder through, then slipped in behind it. It never got near me properly.",
      pyromancer: "Acid glowing in pools, and a hatchling stuck to me, an orange sign over my head! It can't be hurt until it crosses acid. So I strolled round a pool, not through it, and it crossed. Then I set it alight, very prettily.",
      thermist: "Acid pools laid out; each hatchling fixed on the nearest of us, orange sign over the head. Untouchable until it crossed acid. I ran hot and walked straight into a pool once: scalded, slowed. Cold now: round the pool, let it cross, then cut it down.",
      driftblade: "Pools of acid on the floor and a hatchling fixed on me, an orange sign over my head, and it can't be hurt until it crosses acid, so I ran a loop round a pool and the thing went straight through the acid behind me, and then it was dead.",
      marionettist: "Three pools of acid on the stage, and each hatchling takes the nearest player for its partner, an orange sign over their head. It cannot be struck until it has crossed the acid. So I walked a circuit round a pool, mine followed through it, then the curtain fell on it.",
      cleric: "Acid on the floor in three pools, and a hatchling attached itself to each of the nearest of us, who wore an orange sign. Nothing could hurt it until it crossed the acid. I asked them kindly to walk round the pool, not through, and strike it after.",
      grovekeeper: "Acid pooled in three places, and each hatchling fixed on the nearest of us like a seedling to a stake, an orange sign over their head. It cannot be hurt until it has crossed acid. Walk round the pool, dear, let it wade through, then reap it.",
      chronomancer: "Three pools. Each hatchling takes the nearest of us and follows only them, orange sign overhead. It cannot be struck until the acid has touched it. I step round the pool and it wades through; I will have done that before.",
      alembist: "Observation: acid pools on the floor, and a hatchling latched onto one of us, orange sign over the head. Immune until it crosses acid. Method: walk round a pool, not through it, let it cross, then kill it. Note: I tested one pool. Stung.",
      threadweaver: "Three pools of acid, and each hatchling tied itself to whoever stood nearest, an orange sign over their head, a single thread to one stitch. It cannot be hurt until it has crossed acid. Walk round a pool, let it wade through, then cut it.",
      tidecaller: "Three pools of acid lay about the floor, and each hatchling followed one of us, the nearest when it hatched, an orange sign over their head. Nothing harmed it until it had been through the acid. I walked round a pool, as one walks round a rip, and let it come through.",
      lanternkeeper: "Each young one chose a hero by hatching nearest, an orange sign hanging over that head. Mine walked at my heel. I stood so a pool lay between us, lantern raised, and it came straight through the acid. After that it could be struck, and I put it out.",
      kenseier: "Each hatchling chose the nearest of us and held to that one, an orange sign above the head. Steel did nothing until it had crossed the acid. I took a position so a pool lay between us, it came through, and then I cut it down on the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Three pools of acid, and each little one fixed on whoever stood nearest, orange sign over his head. Mine stuck to me like a burr. Can't be hurt till it's crossed acid. Walked round a pool, let it through, then cut it down. Dogs stayed out of the acid.",
    } },
    "Cat's Cradle": { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Two chains over heads, gold and violet, and a silk line between each pair. When the lines cross they scorch all four of us again and again. I kept my ground and the others walked until the lines lay clear. The web tightens at the last, harder for every crossing.",
      gloomsworn: "Chains over four heads, gold on two, violet on two, and a strand drawn between each pair. Where the strands cross, all four are burned, over and over. We walked until they lay apart. The web draws tight at the end, harder the longer they crossed. I took it standing.",
      thresher: "Gold chains over two heads, violet over the other two, and silk drawn between each partnered pair. Crossed, the lines scorch all four, dear, so we twirled about until they ran side by side. The web pulls tight at the end, and the longer we crossed, the harder.",
      bloodreaver: "A chain over four heads, gold and violet, and a line between each pair! Where the lines cross, we all burn, again and again. Someone has to walk until they lie apart. The web snaps tight at the end, and the longer we crossed, the harder it bites.",
      runeguard: "Two pairs, each bound by a silk line, each pair's chains in its own colour, gold or violet. The omen is the lines themselves: where they cross, all four burn, repeatedly. Walk until they lie apart. The web tightens at the end, and every moment crossed adds to its weight.",
      gorgemaw: "Four of us strung up like sausages, two gold, two violet, a silk line to each partner. Where the lines cross, the whole string sizzles. We shuffled until they lay apart. At the end the web pulled tight, harder for every moment it had spent crossed. Overcooked, if we let it.",
      oathbroker: "A joint obligation between four: two pairs, chains gold and violet over our heads, a silk line binding each pair. Where the lines cross, all four pay, repeatedly. We moved until they lay apart. The web tightens at term's end, the penalty rising with each moment crossed.",
      ironpalm: "Four chains, two gold, two violet, and silk between each pair. When the lines crossed, all four of us burned. We moved until they did not. The web drew tight at the end, and what it asked was greater for every moment we had crossed. Cross less, pay less.",
      ranger: "Four of us tied, pairs by colour, gold and violet. A silk line between each pair. Where the lines cross, everyone burns. Walked until the lines lay apart, no sooner. The web draws tight at the end; the longer they crossed, the harder it hits. Be mended before it does.",
      shadowblade: "She tied me to a partner and the other two to each other, a gold chain over one pair and violet over the other. Crossed lines burn all four, which is crude. We shuffled until they lay clear. The web tightens at the end, harder the longer they crossed. Someone was careless.",
      pyromancer: "Chains over all four of us, and a silk line between each pair! When the lines cross, we all sizzle, and not in a flattering way. So one pair walks until they lie apart. The web snaps tight at the end, and every moment crossed made it worse. Heal before, please.",
      thermist: "Two pairs, a chain each, gold and violet; a line between. Crossed lines burn us all. Walked until they lay apart. Too slow and it grows hot: the web tightens at the end, harder for every moment the lines crossed. Clear early, then stay clear. Mend before the end.",
      driftblade: "Pairs, tied, gold and violet, silk between, and when the lines crossed we all burned, so I ran my partner round the cave until they lay apart and then stood still, grudgingly. At the end the web yanked tight, harder the longer we had been crossed.",
      marionettist: "Four players tied in two pairs, gold chains and violet chains, a silk line between each pair. When the lines cross, the whole cast is burned. So the company blocks itself apart until the lines lie clear. At the curtain the web tightens, harder for every moment crossed.",
      cleric: "Chains over four of us, gold and violet, and a silk line joining each pair. Where the lines cross, all four are burned, so we walked until they lay apart. At the end the web tightened, harder for every moment they had crossed. I kept everyone mended before it came.",
      grovekeeper: "Two pairs tied with silk, gold chains and violet, like vines trained apart, dear. Where the vines cross, all four of us wither, over and over. We shifted until they lay clear. The web tightens at the end, harder for each moment crossed. I planted mending in my lot beforehand.",
      chronomancer: "Two pairs, bound by lines: gold chains, violet chains. When the lines cross, all four burn on the beat, again and again. Walk until they do not. At the end the web tightens, harder for every beat it spent crossed. I will have uncrossed them sooner. I did, the second time.",
      alembist: "Problem: four of us tied in two pairs, chains gold and violet, a line between each pair. Crossed lines burn all four. Solution: walk until they lie apart. Note: the web tightens at the end, harder the longer they crossed. Mend everyone beforehand; brew nothing late.",
      threadweaver: "She tied my stitches in two pairs, gold and violet, and ran a line between each. Crossed lines burn all four, a snag of the worst kind. We walked until they lay apart, a neat parallel. The web tightens at the end, harder for every moment it had snarled.",
      tidecaller: "Four of us were tied in two pairs, gold chains and violet, with a silk line between each. When the lines crossed, all four burned, again and again. We walked slowly until they lay apart. The web drew tight at the end, harder for every swell it had crossed.",
      lanternkeeper: "Four chains over four of us, two gold, two violet, one silk line to each partner. I lit the floor where each pair needed to stand so the lines would run apart. Crossed lines burn everyone, and the web draws tight at the end, hardest if they crossed long.",
      kenseier: "Two pairs, tied by silk, a chain over each head, gold and violet. Where the lines cross, all four burn, beat after beat. We walked until they lay apart. At the end the web draws tight, harder for every beat spent crossed. A line I could not cut, only clear.",
      houndmaster: "Four of us tied in pairs, gold and violet chains over our heads, silk between. Lines cross, everybody burns. Dogs stayed clear of it; the four of us walked till the lines lay apart. Web tightens at the end, worse for every moment crossed. Mend first.",
    } },
    'Silk Reel': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She hooked whoever stood farthest from her and reeled them in. A quick bound away the moment it pulls snaps the thread; reaching her means a bite. I keep her ready for whoever arrives, but nobody should arrive.",
      gloomsworn: "She hooked the farthest of us and pulled. A sharp leap away just as it tightens snaps the thread. Reach her, and she bites. I waited for whoever came, though nobody should have.",
      thresher: "She casts a thread at whoever stands farthest and draws them in like a hooked fish. The far hands leaned against it, then jumped away sharp as it pulled, and it snapped. Come all the way in and she bites. A dance of its own, that.",
      bloodreaver: "She reels in whoever's farthest, for a bite! Lucky them. They don't want it, though, so they pull away and then leap hard, and the thread snaps.",
      runeguard: "A thread fastens on whoever is farthest. One sharp leap away as the pull begins snaps it; struggling against it only slows it. Arriving at her means a venomous bite. I recommend not arriving.",
      gorgemaw: "Fishing for the guest farthest from the table and reeling them in. They leap away to snap the line. Arrive, and she takes a bite. Rude, eating one's guests.",
      oathbroker: "A thread on whoever is farthest: a summons to her mouth. Pulling away delays it; a sharp leap quashes the summons outright. On harder nights, the summons also names our healer.",
      ironpalm: "She hooked the farthest and pulled. They pulled back, then leapt. The thread snapped.",
      ranger: "Hooked me. Farthest away, of course. Pulled against it to slow it, then a quick leap, and the thread snapped. Still counted my paces to the wall.",
      shadowblade: "She hooked the farthest of us. Never me; I am right behind her. Our archer leapt away as the thread pulled, and it snapped. Dragged all the way in, one gets bitten.",
      pyromancer: "She hooked me with silk and started reeling! I leapt away sharp as the pull began, and the thread snapped. Being dragged all the way in means a bite. Not glamorous.",
      thermist: "Hooked, farthest; walk away to slow it, one leap to snap it. I froze, she dragged, I panicked. Leapt. Snapped. Too close, and a fever of a moment.",
      driftblade: "She hooked me from across the cave and began dragging, so I pulled against it and then leapt away through the air and the thread snapped clean, and I was gone.",
      marionettist: "She fishes for the back row with a line of silk. The hooked performer pulls against it, then leaps away, and the line snaps. (Dragged onstage means bitten.)",
      cleric: "She hooks whoever stands farthest and reels them in for a bite; on harder nights, she hooks me as well. A quick leap away snaps the thread. If the bite lands anyway, I mend it.",
      grovekeeper: "A thread caught me out at the edge and began to reel. Pull against it to slow it, dear; one sharp bound away and it snaps, like a weed's root. Reaching her ends in a bite.",
      chronomancer: "A thread on whoever is farthest; she pulls after a beat. Leap on that beat and it snaps. Reaching her is a bite. On the hard nights she hooks me too, which is personal.",
      alembist: "A thread on whoever's farthest, reeling them in for a venomous bite. Pull away to slow it, one sharp leap to snap it. Note: on harder nights, it takes me too. The only time I have been fished for.",
      threadweaver: "She hooks the stitch farthest out and reels them in. Pull against it, then one sharp leap, and it snaps. Arriving at her ends in a bite. On harder nights she hooks me too, and I do not enjoy being on the needle.",
      tidecaller: "A thread hooked the farthest of us and began to haul. One sharp leap away, just as it pulls, snaps it before she can bite. On the hard nights it hooked me as well, which I took as a compliment to my distance.",
      lanternkeeper: "A thread hooked the farthest of us and began to haul. A sharp leap away, just as it pulls, snaps it before she can bite. On the hard nights it found me too. I leapt, and set my lantern down where I landed.",
      kenseier: "She hooked whoever stood farthest and reeled them in. A sharp leap away just as it tightens snaps the thread. The beat of it is the pull itself. Reach her, and she bites.",
      houndmaster: "She hooked me, for I stood furthest. Pulled against it, then a sharp leap as it tightened, and the thread snapped. Better yet, don't stand furthest.",
    } },
    'Acid Deluge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She went to the middle, reared up and sprayed the whole cave. Only a pillar's shadow is safe, so the line got behind one together. Each pillar that shelters someone takes a crack. Stone gives ground before steel does.",
      gloomsworn: "Acid poured over the cave from the middle. The shade behind a pillar was the one dry place, and we squeezed into it. Stone that shelters us gets scarred; better stone than us.",
      thresher: "She went to the middle and reared, and acid came in over the whole cave like hard rain. Only the shadow behind a pillar stayed dry. We crowded behind one together; each pillar that shelters someone cracks, and stone does not mend. I was glad of my hat.",
      bloodreaver: "Acid everywhere! I wanted to taste it. The others dragged me behind a pillar, out of her line from the middle. Every pillar that hides someone cracks, so we shared one.",
      runeguard: "She scuttles to the hub and rears. Place a pillar between yourself and her before the acid falls. Every pillar used is chipped, and enough uses bring it down; share them sparingly, as one shares a good reference.",
      gorgemaw: "The whole dining room doused in vinegar, from the middle. A pillar between you and her keeps you dry. Each pillar used takes a splash, so we crowded behind one. Far too acidic.",
      oathbroker: "A levy upon all who stand in her sight from the middle. A pillar between oneself and her is exemption. Each pillar used pays a share, and enough shares bring it down. I took the sturdy one.",
      ironpalm: "She went to the middle and spoke acid in every direction. We stood behind a pillar, where her voice did not reach.",
      ranger: "It went to the middle and reared. Got a pillar between us; pillar, me, then it. Standard hiding. Acid hit everything but the shadow behind it.",
      shadowblade: "Off to the middle for a spray over the whole room. I took shelter behind a pillar, out of her sight. Each pillar used is chipped, and they do not last for ever. Hiding is a craft; stone is only the venue.",
      pyromancer: "Acid over the whole cave, and the loveliest green I have ever hated! I hid behind a pillar with the others, out of her sight from the middle. Each sheltering pillar cracks a little. My boots hissed.",
      thermist: "She's central; a pillar between us; share the shelter. Then acid on every side, roaring. The pillar cracked. On the hard nights the floor stays sticky after.",
      driftblade: "She went to the middle and sprayed the whole cave, and I ran behind a pillar with the others, the only dry ground, and waited there, perfectly still, which cost me more than the acid would have.",
      marionettist: "Centre stage, and she sprays the whole house. The company hides behind the scenery, out of her sightline. Each pillar used is chipped. (The set took a beating.)",
      cleric: "She sprays acid over the whole cave from the middle; only a pillar's shadow is safe. We gathered behind one together, and I mended whoever was splashed. Thank the light for stone.",
      grovekeeper: "Acid came down from the middle like a squall. Only a pillar's lee was dry, dear. My lot gathered behind one; each sheltering pillar is weathered by it, so we shared.",
      chronomancer: "She moves to the middle and rears; a beat later, acid everywhere but behind the pillars. Each pillar used cracks, so we shared one, which I will have suggested.",
      alembist: "Acid over the whole cave, from the middle. A pillar between you and her. Note: each sheltering pillar chips. On harder nights the floor stays sticky after. Spoiled, and I lost a sleeve to it.",
      threadweaver: "Acid from the middle, everywhere but behind the pillars. I gathered my stitches behind one, close. Each pillar used is chipped, so I chose the sturdy one.",
      tidecaller: "She went to the middle, reared, and rained acid on the whole cave. The only dry place was the lee of a pillar, so we crowded behind one together. Each pillar that shelters us wears down, so I chose a sound one.",
      lanternkeeper: "She went to the middle, reared, and rained acid across the cave. The only dry place was the lee of a pillar, so I set a lantern behind a sound one and we crowded into its glow. A pillar that has sheltered us once wears thin. I chose one that had not.",
      kenseier: "She reared in the middle and sprayed the whole cave. The only dry place was a pillar's shade, so we all packed in behind one together. The pillar took the scar. A pillar is a fine shield, and never complains.",
      houndmaster: "She reared in the middle and sprayed the cave. Only dry spot was a pillar's shade, so we packed in behind one together, dogs underneath. Pillar takes the scar.",
    } },
    'Into the Dark': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She climbed out of reach and the lights failed. Three times an egg in the web swelled and quickened, and what hatched ran at me. I held it on the shield while the close fighters cut it down. The knots and eggs were for the others.",
      gloomsworn: "She went up beyond sight and the light thinned. Three times an egg left in the web split, its glow swelling first, and the hatchling came for me. I took it whole and kept it there while blades cut it down. I have stood in darker places.",
      thresher: "Up she went, and her little guests would not wait: an egg's glow swelled and quickened, then split, and the hatchling ran for our knight. I cut it down beside him, a tidy step, then back to the knots. On the hard nights they come as a pair, and go together.",
      bloodreaver: "She climbed into the dark! Rude. But an egg swelled and split, three times, and the hatchling ran for me. Let it chew, then answer! Hard nights bring a pair on a burning thread; one drops, the other has no time, so both go together. The rest broke knots and eggs.",
      runeguard: "An egg's glow swelled and quickened, and what hatched ran for me, thrice over. I held it; the close fighters cut it down, and the rest broke her knots in the order she would run them, then the eggs. An egg broken first sends nothing.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess went up into the dark but left courses behind: an egg split, glowing, and the hatchling ran for me. I held the plate while the close ones carved it. The rest cut her knots and ate the eggs. An egg eaten first serves nothing.",
      oathbroker: "She retired into the dark, but the clutch left in the web falls due three times: an egg swells, quickens, splits, and the issue runs at me. I accepted delivery on the shield; the close fighters settled it; the rest broke knots, then eggs. An egg broken first delivers nothing.",
      ironpalm: "She went into the dark, and the eggs spoke. A glow swelled, quickened, split. Three times a hatchling ran at me, and I met it and kept it. The close ones answered it. The rest broke the knots and the eggs. An egg broken first says nothing.",
      ranger: "Lost sight of it in the dark. Eggs split three times, glow swelling first, and the hatchlings ran at the knight. Left them to him and the close blades. Shot the knots on the threads, then the eggs. An egg shot first sends nothing.",
      shadowblade: "She vanished into the dark, which is my trick. Three times an egg swelled, glowing, and split, and the hatchling ran for our shield. Rude of it. I took it from behind while he held it. The rest cut knots and eggs.",
      pyromancer: "She climbed into the dark and the lights failed! Three times an egg swelled, brighter and quicker, and split, and something ran for our knight. I left it to him and the close fighters, and burned her knots, then the eggs. An egg burned first hatches nothing.",
      thermist: "She's gone, but the brood is not waiting. An egg glows, quickens, splits: three times a hatchling ran at the shield. Cold, steady: leave it to him and the close blades. Knots first, then eggs. On the hot nights a burning pair; down together, or the second scalds.",
      driftblade: "She climbed into the dark and the eggs split three times, glow swelling first, so I ran through the pools of light to whatever hatched and cut it down beside the shield, then round again to the knots and eggs, not stopping once. Pairs on the hard nights, down together.",
      marionettist: "Exit into the flies, house lights failing. But the eggs left on the set split on cue, three times, their glow swelling first, and the hatchling ran at our leading shield. (He held it; the close players struck it down.) The rest of us struck the set: knots, then eggs.",
      cleric: "She climbed into the dark and the cave dimmed. Three times an egg swelled and split, and the hatchling ran at our knight, so I laid the light on him before each came while the others cut it down and broke her knots and eggs.",
      grovekeeper: "She went up like the sun before a storm, but the clutch left behind ripened on its own, three times: the glow swelling, quickening, splitting. Each hatchling ran at our knight. I planted the mending on him before it did. The rest cut it down, then went on to the knots and clutches.",
      chronomancer: "She climbs into the dark; the eggs split anyway, three times, the glow quickening on the beat before. The hatchling runs at the shield, so I mended him a beat early. The close blades cut it down, the rest break knots, then eggs. I did all this sooner, this time.",
      alembist: "The brood does not wait. An egg glows, quickens, splits, and the hatchling runs at the shield. Mending kept on him, close blades to cut it down, the rest on knots, then eggs. Note: broken first, an egg sends nothing. Label them as you go.",
      threadweaver: "She left her threads and the brood did not wait: three times an egg swelled and split, and the hatchling ran at our knight. I pulled him tight with mending while the close blades cut it down and the rest unpicked her knots, then the eggs. An egg unpicked first sends nothing.",
      tidecaller: "She climbed out of reach, but the brood never waits for a tide. Three times an egg's glow swelled and quickened and it split, and I kept the water high around our knight before each hatchling reached him. The close ones cut it down; the rest took the knots, then the eggs.",
      lanternkeeper: "She climbed out of reach and the cave dimmed. An egg's glow swelled and quickened, and I hung a lantern on our knight before it split, three times, because what hatched ran for him. Close fighters cut it down; the rest took knots and eggs. Hard nights, a burning pair, down together.",
      kenseier: "She climbed out of sight, but the brood did not wait. An egg's glow quickened like a count, then split, and the hatchling ran at me. I met it as it landed, three times, while the close blades cut it down. The rest took the knots, then the eggs.",
      houndmaster: "She climbed out of sight and the lights failed. Egg in the web swells, glows quick, splits, three times, and the little one runs for the shield-man. He holds it; Rust and Ash and I cut it down. The rest do knots, then eggs. Hard nights, a pair on a burning thread. Down together.",
    } },
    'From the Dark': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Her shadow ran a thread and she dropped at its end; we stood clear of the landing. Then she crouched a few breaths and I held fast while the others struck her before she climbed. A cut thread sent her after the farthest of us. Late on she fell on the outermost: stay inward.",
      gloomsworn: "Her shadow ran an unbroken thread and she fell where it ended; we let the landing pass. Then she crouched on the stone a short while, and that was our time to cut her, before she hauled up the silk. A cut thread sent her after the farthest of us. Stay inward.",
      thresher: "Her shadow runs a thread and she lands at its end, so I strolled clear. She stays crouched a few breaths, then up the silk she goes, so that is the hour to cut. A cut thread: she picks the farthest, for a shorter visit. Late on she falls on the outermost. Stay in, dear.",
      bloodreaver: "She dropped at her thread's end, I leapt clear, then leapt right back in! She sits crouched a handful of heartbeats, and every one of them is for hitting; then up the silk she goes. A cut thread and she hunts the farthest, a shorter stay. Late on she falls on the outermost! Stay in!",
      runeguard: "Her shadow runs an uncut thread; she falls where it ends. Stand clear of that landing, then spend each moment she crouches there striking her, for she hauls up the silk again in a few. A cut thread: she hunts the farthest, and stays less. Late on, stay inward.",
      gorgemaw: "She dropped on the far end of a thread like a dish from a height; we left that seat. Then she sat crouched a few mouthfuls long, the only time she was on the table, so carve fast. A cut thread, and she goes for the farthest guest. Sit toward the middle.",
      oathbroker: "She descends at the far end of any uncut thread; vacate the landing. She then remains grounded a few moments, the interval in which every party strikes, before she withdraws up the silk. A voided thread means she pursues the farthest, for a shorter term. In the collapse, remain central.",
      ironpalm: "Her shadow ran a thread and she fell at its end. I stepped out, let the dust fall, then struck her while she crouched, for she climbs again within a few breaths. A cut thread and she came for the farthest, staying less. Near the end she fell on the outermost. I stayed in.",
      ranger: "Shadow ran a thread. Read where it ended, left the landing, then loosed every shaft while she crouched down there. A few moments, then she climbs. Cut thread: she hunts whoever is farthest out, stays shorter. Late on she drops on the outermost. Kept to the middle. No shot wasted.",
      shadowblade: "Her shadow ran a thread and she dropped at the far end. I was already clear. She crouches there a short spell with her back to the dark, so I went in and cut deep before she climbed. A cut thread, and she hunts the farthest. Late on, stay inward.",
      pyromancer: "She dropped at her thread's end and I abandoned the spot mid-spell! Then she crouched there a moment or three, and I emptied everything I had into her before she climbed away. A cut thread sends her after the farthest. Late on she falls on the outermost, so I cast from the middle.",
      thermist: "Shadow along a thread; she lands at its far end; leave. Then she crouches for a short, cold while: hit her hard and quick before she climbs. Cut thread: she hunts whoever stands farthest, and stays shorter. Late on she falls from above on the outermost.",
      driftblade: "Her shadow ran a thread and I ran the other way, out of the landing, then straight back in once she was down. She crouches only a few heartbeats before she climbs, so I cut and slid away. A cut thread, and she hunted whoever was farthest. Late on, stay in.",
      marionettist: "She runs a thread in the flies and drops onto its far end; the company clears that mark. Then she crouches downstage for a few beats, and the whole cast strikes before she is hauled back up into the flies. A cut thread: she hunts the farthest, a shorter scene. Stay centre in the finale.",
      cleric: "Her shadow runs along a thread and she drops where it ends; we left that ground. She then crouches on the stone a few breaths, and I kept my people lit while they struck her before she climbed. A cut thread, and she hunts the farthest. Late on, stay inward.",
      grovekeeper: "Her shadow ran along a thread like a cloud, and she fell like hail where it ended; stand clear. Then she crouched, a short while, and that is the time to strike, saplings, before she climbs. A cut thread, she hunts the farthest of my lot, staying less. Late on, stay inward.",
      chronomancer: "Her shadow slides a thread and she lands at its end; I stood off that spot a beat early. Then she crouches a few breaths, so I hasted the blades into her before she climbs. A cut thread and she hunts the farthest of us. Late on she drops on the outermost. Inward, always.",
      alembist: "She drops at the far end of an uncut thread. Leave the landing. Dosage: strike her hard in the few moments she crouches there, before she climbs the silk. Note: a cut thread makes her hunt the farthest of us, and she stays a shorter while. Late on she falls on the outermost; keep inward.",
      threadweaver: "She runs any thread nobody cut and drops at its far end; I moved my stitches off the landing. She stays crouched a short spell, so I drew the blades in and struck, then let go as she climbed. A cut thread, and she hunts the farthest stitch. Stay inward at the end.",
      tidecaller: "A shadow slid along every thread left whole and she broke on the shore where it ended; we left that ground. She lay crouched a while, the ebb of her, and we struck her then before she climbed. Where a thread was cut she hunted the farthest. Keep to the deep middle.",
      lanternkeeper: "A shadow slid down every thread left whole and she landed at its end, so my lights stayed off that patch. She crouched in the open dark a few breaths, and that was the time to strike, before she climbed. A cut thread sent her after the farthest. Keep the lamps inward.",
      kenseier: "An unbroken thread, and she dropped where it ended; I read the count and stood clear. She crouched there a few breaths: strike on the beat, then step back as she climbs. A cut thread, and she chose the farthest of us, and stayed less. Near the end she fell on the outermost. Stand inward.",
      houndmaster: "Her shadow ran an uncut thread and she dropped where it ended, so we quit that ground. Then she crouched a moment and Rust and Ash and I went in hard before she climbed. Cut the thread and she picks the furthest, and stays less. Late on, keep the dogs in to the middle.",
    } },
    'Lure': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A circle fell, following one of the others, who wore a pink sign over the head. They walked it onto an egg clutch, then ran clear as it set. On eggs, it crushes them. On bare floor, the nearest clutch hatches at once, and the young run at me.",
      gloomsworn: "A circle of her weight followed one of the others, a pink sign over their head. They walked it onto the nearest egg clutch and stepped clear as it set, and the clutch was crushed. Missed, and a clutch split at once and its young came for me. I held them.",
      thresher: "A circle comes down following one of the others, a pink sign over their head. They lead it to an egg clutch, then step out as it sets, and she squashes the clutch. Miss it, dear, and the nearest clutch hatches early, and the young run for our knight.",
      bloodreaver: "A pink sign over one of the soft ones, and the circle followed them! Their work: walk it onto an egg clutch, run when it sets, and she crushes the eggs. Miss, and a clutch bursts early and the brood charges me. I don't mind that. Eggs gone is better.",
      runeguard: "A circle follows the one who wears a pink sign. Lead it onto the egg clutch nearest, the ripening ones first, and step clear as it sets: the clutch is crushed. Lead it onto bare floor and the nearest clutch splits at once, its young running to the knight.",
      gorgemaw: "A great plate descended, following the guest with a pink sign over their head. Steer it onto an egg clutch, then leave the table as it settles, and she squashes the lot. Land it on bare floor and the nearest clutch bursts, and the young come for me. Messy.",
      oathbroker: "A circle of her weight follows the party bearing a pink sign. The bearer's duty: lead it to an egg clutch, the ripening ones first, and quit the circle as it sets. The clutch is thereby void. Failing that, the nearest hatches at once, and the issue runs at me.",
      ironpalm: "The pink sign meant a circle was coming for whoever wore it. They led it over an egg clutch and slipped out before it set, and the eggs were crushed. If it fell on empty floor, a clutch hatched in haste and the young ran at me.",
      ranger: "Pink sign over one of the others. The circle follows them. Walked it onto an egg clutch, ripest first, then out before it set. Clutch crushed. Miss, and the nearest clutch hatches early, young run at the knight. I kept clear of the circle.",
      shadowblade: "The circle followed whoever wore the pink sign over their head, which was not me. They led it to an egg clutch and stepped out as it set, and she crushed the eggs for us. Missing it hatches the nearest clutch, young and all.",
      pyromancer: "A pink sign over one of us and a great circle chasing after! They led it onto an egg clutch and leapt clear as it set, and splat. If it falls on bare floor, the nearest clutch hatches at once and the young charge the knight. I stayed out of it.",
      thermist: "A pink sign over one of us; the circle follows them. Walk it onto an egg clutch, then out before it sets: crushed, cold and clean. Miss and the nearest clutch splits at once, hot, and the young run at the shield. I kept off the circle.",
      driftblade: "A pink sign over one of us and the circle chased them round the cave, so they ran it onto an egg clutch and out the far side as it set, and the eggs went to pulp. On bare floor, the nearest clutch splits and the young run at the knight.",
      marionettist: "A pink sign over one player, like a spotlight, and the circle follows them across the stage. Their cue: lead it onto an egg clutch, then exit as it sets, and the eggs are crushed. Miss, and the nearest clutch hatches early, the young charging our leading man.",
      cleric: "A pink sign sat over one of our people, and the circle followed them. I asked them to lead it onto an egg clutch and step out as it set, and the eggs were crushed. On bare floor, the nearest clutch would have hatched and run at our knight.",
      grovekeeper: "A circle followed whoever wore the pink sign, like a cloud over one field. They led it onto an egg clutch, the ripest first, and stepped out as it set, trampling it before it hatched. Over bare ground the nearest clutch bursts, dear, and the young run at our shield.",
      chronomancer: "A circle follows the one with the pink sign until just before it falls, then holds. Lead it onto an egg clutch and step out as it sets; the eggs are crushed. Miss, and the nearest clutch hatches early. I will have led it correctly, and I did.",
      alembist: "Hazard: a circle of her weight, following one who wears a pink sign. Procedure: lead it onto an egg clutch, ripening ones first, and step out as it sets; the clutch is crushed. Note: if it lands on bare floor, the nearest clutch hatches at once. Spoiled stock, and angry.",
      threadweaver: "A pink sign over one of my stitches, and the circle tugged after them like a thread. They drew it onto an egg clutch and stepped out as it set, and the eggs were crushed. Missed, and the nearest clutch splits and runs for our knight.",
      tidecaller: "A circle followed one of us, a pink sign over their head, and I let them lead it where it was needed: onto an egg clutch, then out as it settled. The clutch was crushed. On bare floor it breaks the nearest clutch early, and the young rush the knight.",
      lanternkeeper: "A pink sign over one of us, and the circle followed them about. They carried it to an egg clutch and stepped out as it set. Crushed. On bare floor, the nearest clutch hatched at once and the young ran at the knight. I lit the clutch they aimed for.",
      kenseier: "The circle followed one of the others, a pink sign over their head, until the last beat. They led it onto an egg clutch and stepped out as it set. Crushed. A circle on bare floor splits the nearest clutch, and the young come for me, and I meet them.",
      houndmaster: "Pink sign over one lad's head, circle chasing him round. He walked it onto an egg clutch and ran out as it set. Eggs crushed. Land it on bare floor and the nearest clutch splits at once, young for the shield-man. Dogs sat out of the circle.",
    } },
    'Cocoon Drop': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A cocoon fell on one of the others, never me, and its weight wanted the whole line: all but one on ordinary nights, every soldier on the hard ones. We closed on whoever it was. The husk stays where it landed, cover against the acid for a time. Stone lasts longer.",
      gloomsworn: "A cocoon landed on one of the young ones, never me. All but one must stand under it, on the harder nights every one. The husk stays where it fell, and sheltered us at the next acid, though it gave out quickly. A small grave, but a useful one.",
      thresher: "A cocoon comes down on one of the others, never our knight, and we go in to share the weight, all but one at most, every hand on the hard nights. The husk stays where it fell and makes a fine little shelter from the acid, dear. Do be quick; it cracks.",
      bloodreaver: "A cocoon dropped on one of the soft ones and we piled in, all but one on gentle nights, all of us on hard! The husk stays behind, solid, a decent wall against the acid. A couple of soakings and it's rubble, though. I gave it a few extra, out of spite.",
      runeguard: "A cocoon settles on one of the party, never the knight. All but one gather on them, every one on the harder nights. The husk remains where it fell, solid, and serves at the next acid as a pillar would, though a lesser one; it fails after two uses.",
      gorgemaw: "A cocoon, swung down on one guest, and the rest of us expected at that table: all but one on a gentle night, every seat on a hard one. The husk stays on the floor after, and is something to shelter behind when the acid comes. Briefly.",
      oathbroker: "A cocoon descends upon one of the company, never the shield. The others attend, all but one on ordinary terms, all of us on the hard. A rider: the husk remains, and shelters us from the next acid, but it is a short lease and lapses after two uses.",
      ironpalm: "A cocoon fell on one of us. The rest went to them, all but one, or all of us on the harder nights. I went in. The husk stayed, hard as a stone. When the acid spoke, I stood behind it. It held twice, then broke.",
      ranger: "Cocoon over one of the others, not the knight. Everybody in on them, all but one if the night's easy. It leaves a husk. Solid. Cover for the acid, though only a couple of times. Noted where it lay.",
      shadowblade: "A cocoon dropped on one of the others, and I was invited. Everyone in, or nearly; everyone on the hard nights. The husk stays behind, a pillar of sorts for the next acid, though it breaks soon. I use whatever hides me.",
      pyromancer: "A cocoon fell on one of us, not the knight, and everyone had to run to share it! All but one, usually, all of us on the hard nights. The husk stays after, a little pillar for the acid, and soon cracks. Short-lived, like most of my best shelters.",
      thermist: "Cocoon over someone, not the shield. All in, close; on an ordinary night one may lag, on a hard one none. The husk stays after, cold and solid, cover for the acid. It cracks quickly, so hide behind it, and not for long.",
      driftblade: "A cocoon dropped on one of us and the rest ran to them, all but one, or every one on the hard nights. I was already moving. Then out through the gap, but the husk it left stays solid, which suits me: something to dodge behind when the acid comes.",
      marionettist: "A cocoon descends on one player, never the lead. The company gathers on their mark, all but one in an easy house, every one in a hard one. The husk stays onstage as scenery, good cover when the acid rains, for a couple of showings. (The Doll was not consulted.)",
      cleric: "A cocoon fell on one of my friends, never our knight, and the rest of us went to share it, all but one on an ordinary night, every one on a hard one. The husk it left is solid and shelters us from the acid, though not for long.",
      grovekeeper: "A cocoon dropped on one of my lot like a seed pod, dear, and the rest drew in around it, all but one on an easy night, all of us on a hard one. The husk lies where it fell and shelters you from the acid until it breaks.",
      chronomancer: "A cocoon over one of the party, never the shield. All gather on it; one may be missing on an ordinary night, none on a harder one. The husk stays behind as cover, and I will have used it for the acid, which it survives only twice.",
      alembist: "A cocoon over one of us, never the knight. Everyone to them, all but one on ordinary nights, all of us on the harder. Note: a single absentee costs everyone. Residue: the husk stays, solid, a small shelter for the acid. Two doses, and it spoils.",
      threadweaver: "A cocoon dropped over one of my stitches, never the knight. I gathered the rest around it, all but one, or all of them on the harder nights, close as buttons on a placket. The husk stays as a little wall for the acid. It frays after two uses.",
      tidecaller: "A cocoon fell on one of us, never the knight, and its weight wanted sharing. All but one went in under it on an ordinary night, every last one on the hard nights. The husk stayed like a stone on a beach, and I took shelter behind it when the acid came.",
      lanternkeeper: "Something heavy came down on one of us, never the knight, and it wanted company. On an ordinary night all but one stood beneath it; on the hard nights, all. I set a lantern on the spot first. What it left is a small wall against acid, for a while.",
      kenseier: "A cocoon fell on one of the others, and its weight wanted all of us beneath it but one; on the hard nights, every last one. I went in with the rest. The husk stayed where it fell, a shield that breaks after two uses.",
      houndmaster: "A cocoon came down on one of the others. All of us went under it but one on easy nights, every one on hard. Called the dogs in with me. The husk stays where it dropped, and does for cover at the acid, a bit. Cracks soon.",
    } },
    'Venom Drip': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Venom gathered over each of our heads and followed us. Every one of us needed our own patch of floor. The line opened out, kept its distance until it fell, and closed again after. Orderly, for a retreat.",
      gloomsworn: "Venom pooled above each of us and followed. We each wanted an empty place, so we scattered, let it fall, and then came back together.",
      thresher: "Venom darkened over each of our heads and followed us about. Each of us took our own patch of ground, well apart, so none fell on two, and we came back in after it dropped. A very polite sort of rain.",
      bloodreaver: "Venom over every head! It burns you and anyone close by. I took mine alone, well away from the others, a clean little scald. Then straight back in.",
      runeguard: "Venom gathers over every head at once, and it follows. Each to their own patch of floor, well apart, until it falls; then the party closes again. A paragraph each, and no sharing of margins.",
      gorgemaw: "A drip of venom over every guest's head. One portion each, to be taken alone; share a plate and you get both helpings. We each retired to our own corner. A sour little amuse-bouche.",
      oathbroker: "A charge upon each party severally, not jointly. Stand near another and you pay their share as well. We parted, paid, and reconvened.",
      ironpalm: "Venom over every head. Each of us alone with it. We stepped apart and waited. It fell. Then we came together.",
      ranger: "Venom over each of us. Took our own ground, well apart.",
      shadowblade: "Venom over everyone's head, which was impolite. We each slipped off to our own patch of floor, and mine was, as ever, in the dark behind the mark.",
      pyromancer: "Venom gathered over all our heads! I swept off to my own little stage, well away from everyone, and let it drip on me alone. Somewhat dissolved, still dazzling.",
      thermist: "Venom over each head; step apart; your own ground. I drifted near the archer and we both scalded. Too close. Apart, then together once it fell.",
      driftblade: "Venom gathered over every head, so we each flew to our own patch, mine the farthest, through the gap and out. I waited for the drip, and came straight back.",
      marionettist: "Venom over every head in the company. Each performer takes a solo spot, well apart. (A rare scene where nobody shares the stage, least of all the Doll.)",
      cleric: "Venom gathered over each of our heads. Everyone needed their own patch of floor, apart, so nobody was struck twice. I mended them one by one after.",
      grovekeeper: "Venom over every head, like rain from separate clouds. My lot each found their own patch of ground, well apart, dear, so no cloud rained on two. Then we drew in again.",
      chronomancer: "Venom over every head; it falls on a slow count, and it follows. We each took our own patch, apart, and drifted back together on the beat after. I had known.",
      alembist: "Venom over every head at once. Everyone on their own patch of floor, apart, and the mending thrown into the middle after. Note: let no two doses mix. I have the scorched ceiling to prove it.",
      threadweaver: "Venom over each stitch's head. I cut the threads and sent them apart, each to their own patch, then tied them all back together after.",
      tidecaller: "Venom gathered over every head and followed us. Each took a patch of floor to themselves, well apart, so no drop fell on two. Then we drew in again, and I raised the water as we closed.",
      lanternkeeper: "Venom hung over every head and followed us about. Each of us took a patch of floor apart from the rest, so no drop fell on two, and then we drew back in toward my lantern. Four patches, four of us.",
      kenseier: "Venom gathered above each of us and followed. Each wanted floor of their own, so we scattered, let it fall, and came back together. I kept moving until it landed. Stillness belongs to the other beats.",
      houndmaster: "Venom gathered over each of us and followed. Everyone to their own ground, spread like beaters, then back together once it fell. Moved the dogs along with me. Still dogs get dripped on.",
    } },
    'Tightening Web': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Down she came in the middle, and the landing went through every one of us. The outer web stayed sticky from then on, so the line fights within the rim. Ground lost, and accounted for.",
      gloomsworn: "She landed in the centre and the shock went through all of us. The outer web was sticky from then on, so we fought within the rim and no further.",
      thresher: "She came down in the middle and the landing rolled through all of us; I was topped up for it, so it hardly counted, and I hummed on. The outer web stayed sticky afterwards, like wet clay, so I kept to the inner floor.",
      bloodreaver: "She dropped back into the middle and shook the whole cave! Felt it in my teeth. Lovely. Then the outer web went sticky, so I keep the old mother well away from the rim.",
      runeguard: "Her shadow runs back to the hub. Be whole when she lands, for the blow reaches everyone; then keep inward, for the outer web stays sticky to the end. The page has a smaller margin after that, and we must write within it.",
      gorgemaw: "She returned to the table with a thump that rattled every plate in the cave. Then she glazed the edges of the room in syrup. We dine in the middle from now on.",
      oathbroker: "She returns to the middle and levies a charge on all of us upon landing. A further clause: the outer web stays sticky for the remainder of the term. We conduct our business inside it.",
      ironpalm: "She came back to the middle and spoke once, to all of us. We were ready. Then the edges grew sticky, so we stayed near the middle.",
      ranger: "It came back to the middle. Landing hit everyone. Outer web sticky after. Lost my long lanes. Kept inside the rim.",
      shadowblade: "Back to the middle with a thump that reached everyone. After that the outer web turned sticky, so I keep to the inner floor now, and to her back.",
      pyromancer: "She landed in the middle and shook every one of us! Then the outer web turned sticky, for good. I have kept inside the rim ever since, which ruins all my best long-distance angles.",
      thermist: "She lands in the middle; everyone takes it; be whole first. Then the outer web stays sticky. I got caught on the rim and nearly boiled over. Back to the middle. Literally.",
      driftblade: "She came back to the middle and the landing shook us all, and then the outer web went sticky, which ruins half my runs, so I run the inner circle now, round and through.",
      marionettist: "A return to centre stage with a crash the whole house feels. Then the outer stage turns to glue, and the company keeps to the inner boards for the rest of the run.",
      cleric: "She dropped into the middle and the landing reached every one of us; I had mended them beforehand. After that the outer web stayed sticky, so I kept my friends inside the rim.",
      grovekeeper: "She came down into the middle like the first storm of autumn, felt in every field. I planted before it. After, the outer web turned sticky as a bog, dear, and we kept to the inner ground.",
      chronomancer: "Her shadow runs home to the middle, and the landing reaches everyone; be whole ahead of it. Afterward the outer web is sticky for the rest of the song. I stayed inside it.",
      alembist: "Her landing in the middle, felt by all; then the outer web turns sticky for good. Everyone topped up before she lands, and inside the rim after. Note: the rim is spoiled. Do not store anything there.",
      threadweaver: "She came back to the middle and the landing tugged on every stitch. After that the outer web went sticky, so I keep my stitches gathered inside it.",
      tidecaller: "She came down in the middle and the landing went through all of us; I had the water high beforehand. After that the outer web stayed sticky as pitch on a hull, so I sang from inside the rim, as did everyone.",
      lanternkeeper: "She dropped into the middle, and the landing reached all of us, so I let every lantern burst as she fell. After that the outer web turned sticky. I moved my lights inward and kept the party inside the rim.",
      kenseier: "She landed in the middle and the shock ran through all of us. The outer web was sticky from then on, so we fought inside the rim and no farther. Less floor, fewer places to be wrong.",
      houndmaster: "She came back down in the middle and the shock ran through us all. The outer web's sticky from then on, so we kept inside the rim, dogs included. Less floor, fewer mistakes.",
    } },
    'Spinneret Volley': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Silk rained on everyone but me, a glob at a time, each following its target until just before it fell. They kept moving, apart from each other, and I kept her still. Hard nights only; the line learns to walk.",
      gloomsworn: "Everyone but me was showered with silk, globs that tracked their marks until the last moment. They kept apart and kept moving. Mine was to keep her still while they ran. The harder nights bring this one.",
      thresher: "On the hard nights silk comes at everyone but the knight, a glob each, tracking right up to the last. I walked a long slow curve apart from the rest, as if in a dance, and every glob fell where I had been. In time, dears!",
      bloodreaver: "Silk globs falling on everyone but me! Insulting. The others keep walking, apart, and each glob chases its owner, settles a moment before it drops, and lands on empty floor.",
      runeguard: "Silk gathers over all but whoever holds her. Keep walking, apart, for each glob follows its mark until a breath before it falls, and they come in a long train. I paced the room in a wide, sedate oval, and was never once caught.",
      gorgemaw: "Silk sprinkled on every guest but me, glob after glob, like a cook seasoning plates. They walked on and left each glob on an empty seat. I was not served. Offended.",
      oathbroker: "A series of instalments against all but myself, each following its debtor until just before it falls due. The debtors keep walking, apart, and each lands where they were.",
      ironpalm: "Silk fell on the others, again and again. Each followed until just before. They walked steadily. Each landed behind them.",
      ranger: "Silk on all of us but the knight. Each glob tracks you, then stops just before. Kept walking, steady, wide of the others.",
      shadowblade: "Silk rained on everyone but our knight, glob after glob, each following until just before it fell. I strolled on, apart, and left them landing in my wake. Terribly dull of her.",
      pyromancer: "Silk raining on me, glob after glob, each one tracking me until just before! I kept walking steadily, apart from the others, casting what I could on the move. My staff is mostly silk now.",
      thermist: "Globs on all but the knight; each follows, then settles; keep walking, apart. I stopped to cast once and was glued to the floor. Keep moving, keep cool.",
      driftblade: "Silk fell on us one glob after another, each chasing me until just before it dropped, so I ran a long easy circle, wide of the others, and every one landed behind me.",
      marionettist: "Silk rains on the company, glob after glob, each following its performer until just before. The company keeps walking, well apart. The knight, untouched, looked bored.",
      cleric: "On harder nights silk rains on all of us but our knight, glob after glob, each following until just before. We kept walking steadily, apart, and I mended whoever was caught.",
      grovekeeper: "Silk fell on us like sleet, each glob following one of us until just before it landed. My lot kept walking, steady and apart, dear, and the sleet fell behind them.",
      chronomancer: "Globs fall on all but the knight, in a steady march. Each follows until an instant before it lands. Keep walking, apart, in tempo, and they fall behind you.",
      alembist: "Silk globs on all but our knight, each tracking its target until just before. Keep walking steadily, apart. Note: stand still and you are glued to the bench. I have been glued to a bench.",
      threadweaver: "Silk fell on every stitch but the knight, glob after glob, each following until just before. We kept walking, apart, the thread let out long.",
      tidecaller: "Silk rained on all of us but the knight, each glob following its mark until the last moment. We walked, steady and apart, as on shingle with the tide coming in, and let every one fall behind us.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights silk rained on all of us but the knight, each glob following its mark until the last moment. We kept walking, apart, and let them fall behind us. I walked with the rest and left a light trailing, and nothing caught me standing still.",
      kenseier: "Silk rained on everyone but me, globs that tracked their marks until the last moment. They kept moving and kept apart, and I kept her looking at me. My part was to be a still target. Theirs was to be a hard one.",
      houndmaster: "Silk on all of us but the shield-man, each glob chasing its mark and stopping just short. Kept walking, steady, away from the others. Whistled the dogs clear as well.",
    } },
    'Molt': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights she shed her skin, and the husk struck her last pattern of legs again while she began a new one elsewhere. I kept the old pattern in mind and watched both. A husk is a shield with no one behind it.",
      gloomsworn: "On the harder nights she shed her skin, and the empty shell repeated her last strikes where it stood while she began new ones. Two spiders, one old pattern in my head. I know how a husk feels.",
      thresher: "She sheds her skin, on the hard nights, and the husk strikes again the pattern she last made while she starts a fresh one. An old dance beneath a new one, how nice. I kept the old pattern in my head and watched both.",
      bloodreaver: "She left her old skin behind and it fought on without her! The husk repeats her last strikes while she starts fresh ones. Twice the old mother! A good night.",
      runeguard: "She sheds her skin and leaves the husk standing. It repeats her previous strikes from where it stands while she begins anew; remember the last order, read the new. A palimpsest with teeth.",
      gorgemaw: "She left her shell on the table and it served the last course again, from where it lay, while she began a new one. Reheated leftovers. I watched both plates.",
      oathbroker: "She sheds her skin, and the husk executes her previous terms again while she begins fresh ones. One must recall the last contract while reading the new.",
      ironpalm: "She shed her skin. The skin repeated her last words while she spoke new ones. I listened to both.",
      ranger: "Shed her skin. Husk replays her last strikes from where it stands. She starts new ones. Watch both.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard nights the mark sheds its skin, and the husk repeats its last strikes while the mark begins new ones. Twice the eyes, and still neither looking for me.",
      pyromancer: "She shed her skin and the husk repeated her last strikes while she started fresh ones. I kept the old pattern in my head and found the gaps in both. Two spiders, one very patient spark.",
      thermist: "The husk replays her last strikes; she starts new ones; read both. Legs on every side and I could not breathe. Remembered the last pattern. Found the gap. Cooled.",
      driftblade: "She shed her skin and the husk replayed her last strikes while she began new ones, so I ran the gaps of both at once, through and round, and landed on neither.",
      marionettist: "A costume left onstage, where it repeats her last scene while she begins the next. The company must watch both performances. (The Doll preferred the first.)",
      cleric: "On harder nights she sheds her skin, and the husk strikes as she struck last while she begins afresh. We remembered the last pattern and watched both, and I prayed for sharper eyes.",
      grovekeeper: "She shed her skin like a snake in spring, and the husk repeated her last strikes while she started new ones. My lot remembered the old pattern and watched both, dear.",
      chronomancer: "She leaves her husk behind, and it repeats her last strikes while she plays new ones: the same tune sung as a round. Remember the last pattern; it comes again.",
      alembist: "On harder nights her husk repeats her last strikes while she begins new ones. Remember the last pattern, read the new. Note: keep a label on each. The husk is the one that looks cleaner.",
      threadweaver: "She left her husk behind and it stitched her last strikes over again while she began new ones. I kept the old pattern in my head and watched both.",
      tidecaller: "She slipped out of her skin, and the empty shell swung her last pattern of legs again, on the spot, while she started a fresh one. I kept the old one in mind the way one keeps a tide table, and read both at once.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights she slipped her skin, and the empty husk swung her last pattern of legs again where it lay, while she began a new one. I held the old pattern as I would the order of a row of lamps, and watched both.",
      kenseier: "She shed her skin, and the husk repeated her last strikes from where it stood while she began new ones elsewhere. I kept the old pattern in mind and watched both spiders. An echo has its own beat, a little late.",
      houndmaster: "She shed her skin, and the husk repeated her last blows from where it stood while she started new ones elsewhere. Watched both of them. The old one's slow and a little late.",
    } },
    'Ceiling Ambush': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights she climbed into the ceiling and her shadow crossed to where one of us stood. Her landing was drawn on the floor, plain as a chalk mark. We left it; whoever stays is thrown into the walls.",
      gloomsworn: "On the harder nights she vanished into the roof and her shadow crossed to where one of us had stood. Where she would land was drawn on the floor. We left it bare; whoever is in it is hurled into the walls.",
      thresher: "On the hard nights she climbs into the roof, and her shadow crosses to where one of us stood. The landing shows on the floor. I stepped clear before it came due; whoever stays in it gets thrown to the walls.",
      bloodreaver: "Up into the ceiling she went, and her shadow ran to where one of us stood. I stayed in the circle once. Thrown into the wall! It rattled my teeth. Now I step out.",
      runeguard: "She climbs into the ceiling, and her shadow crosses toward where one of us stood. Leave the circle marked where she will land, before she drops and throws us to the walls. I footnoted the circle.",
      gorgemaw: "Into the rafters, then down onto a guest's empty chair. Her landing is marked; we left it. Those who lingered were thrown into the walls like spoiled crockery.",
      oathbroker: "She ascends, and her shadow travels to where one of the company stood at that moment. The landing is marked; vacate it, or be thrown into the walls.",
      ironpalm: "She climbed into the ceiling. Her shadow crossed to where one of us stood. We left that ground. She fell on nothing.",
      ranger: "Up into the roof. Shadow slid to where one of us had been. Landing drawn on the floor. Out of the circle before she dropped.",
      shadowblade: "Up into the ceiling, then down where one of us stood. The landing is marked on the floor, which rather spoils the surprise. I stepped out of it.",
      pyromancer: "She vanished into the ceiling, and her shadow crossed to where I had stood! The landing glowed on the floor. I left it, and she crashed down where I had been.",
      thermist: "She climbs; her shadow runs to where one of us stood; landing marked; leave it. I was thrown into the wall once. Not again. Cold, quick, out.",
      driftblade: "She climbed into the ceiling and her shadow raced across the floor to where I had stood, so I ran out of the landing and through, and she dropped on nothing.",
      marionettist: "She vanishes into the flies, and her shadow crosses to where one of the company stood. The landing is marked. (The company exits. Nobody is thrown into the scenery.)",
      cleric: "On the harder nights she climbed into the ceiling and her shadow crossed to where one of us stood. The place she would land was marked, and I told everyone to leave it, for whoever stays is flung into the wall.",
      grovekeeper: "She went up into the ceiling like a hawk, dear, and her shadow slid across to where one of us had been standing. Where she would stoop was drawn on the floor. We were off it before she fell; whoever stays is flung to the walls.",
      chronomancer: "Up she goes; the shadow settles on whoever stood still when she left. The landing is drawn on the floor, and I was gone from it before she dropped. A trick of knowing the ending.",
      alembist: "She drops from the ceiling onto where one of us stood. Keep out of the marked landing. Note: anyone left in it is thrown into the walls; nothing on the shelf survives that either.",
      threadweaver: "She climbed into the ceiling and her shadow crossed to where one stitch had stood. The landing was marked. We moved before she dropped, every one of us out of the circle like pins out of a hem.",
      tidecaller: "She went into the ceiling and her shadow slid over to where one of us stood. Her landing was drawn on the floor. We were off it before she fell, since whoever stays is thrown into the walls. There was no need to hurry, only to be elsewhere.",
      lanternkeeper: "Up she went into the ceiling, and her shadow settled over one of us. The ring on the floor was where she would land. The whole party left it, lights and all, because anyone who stays is flung at the walls.",
      kenseier: "She vanished into the roof and her shadow crossed to where one of us stood. Where she would land was drawn on the floor, plain as a line of chalk. We left it bare, for whoever stays is thrown against the wall.",
      houndmaster: "She went up into the roof, and her shadow slid to someone's spot. Landing's drawn on the floor, plain. Left it empty before she dropped. Whoever stays gets thrown. Hounds called in.",
    } },
    'The Swarm': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Every clutch left standing hatched at once, in pairs that must die together. I took them onto me and the line cut each pair level. Every egg we broke earlier was one less to fight. Quartermaster's arithmetic.",
      gloomsworn: "Every clutch left standing split open together. They came in pairs, and each pair must fall together. I drew them onto me and we cut both of each pair down level. Eggs broken earlier gave us nothing to fight.",
      thresher: "Pairs of spiderlings burst from every clutch we had not smashed, and each pair has to fall together or the survivor goes wild. We went through them two at a time, like a country dance. Break the eggs early; the ones we had broken sent none.",
      bloodreaver: "Every egg left in the web hatched at once! Spiderlings everywhere, all teeth! Pairs again; kill one and its twin goes mad. We took them pair by pair. Scars all round.",
      runeguard: "Every clutch left standing ripens together. They hatch in pairs, and each pair must fall together. Fewer clutches spared before means fewer spiderlings now. The consequence of a careless margin.",
      gorgemaw: "The whole nursery served at once: every clutch left on the shelf hatches. Pairs again, to be eaten together. A buffet. The eggs we cracked earlier made it a smaller one, which the others insist is the point.",
      oathbroker: "Every clutch still standing hatches at once, each heir bound to another. Each pair is settled together, or the survivor defaults. Clutches broken earlier are discharged.",
      ironpalm: "Every egg left hatched. They came in pairs. We silenced each pair together.",
      ranger: "Every clutch left hatched at once. Pairs. Each pair brought down together or the survivor runs wild. Fewer eggs earlier, fewer fangs now.",
      shadowblade: "Every egg the mark had left hatched at once. Pairs, and they hate dying alone. We ended each pair together. Fewer eggs, fewer guests.",
      pyromancer: "Every egg we had left hatched at once, and the cave was all legs! Pairs, so I burned each pair down level. The ones we broke earlier were fewer to burn, which was a pity.",
      thermist: "Pairs from every clutch left standing, all at once. Each pair down together. I broke more eggs the next time and the crowd thinned. Cooler for it.",
      driftblade: "Every egg left in the web hatched at once and I ran through them all, round and through, pair by pair, wearing each pair down together.",
      marionettist: "The whole chorus of spiderlings takes the stage at once! Pairs, as ever, and each pair must exit together. (Fewer eggs earlier means a smaller chorus.)",
      cleric: "Her children came out all at once from every egg we had spared, in pairs, and we ended each pair together. Each egg we broke earlier was one less to mourn. I did not look at them for long.",
      grovekeeper: "Every clutch left standing hatched at once, a whole season's growth in one morning. We reaped each pair together, dear. The fewer we left, the lighter the harvest.",
      chronomancer: "Every clutch still standing hatches at once. Pairs, each on the same beat. The fewer we left earlier, the shorter this verse. I will have broken more.",
      alembist: "Every clutch left standing hatches at once. Each pair down together. Note: clutches broken earlier hatch nothing. Fewer jars on the shelf, fewer spiders.",
      threadweaver: "Every clutch still standing hatched at once, each pair joined like a double stitch. We cut each pair together. Fewer eggs earlier meant fewer threads to cut now.",
      tidecaller: "Every clutch we had spared hatched at once, in pairs, and each pair had to fall together. The fewer eggs left standing, the less water I had to spend. We brought each pair down level and ended it in one breath.",
      lanternkeeper: "All the clutches we had spared hatched together, in pairs, and every pair had to be finished at the same moment. Each egg broken earlier was one less lamp to tend afterward. We took each pair down evenly.",
      kenseier: "All the eggs we had left hatched together, in pairs, and a pair must fall as one. I held them on me while the rest cut each pair down level. The eggs we broke earlier never hatched, and I was grateful to our past selves.",
      houndmaster: "Every clutch we left hatched at once, in pairs, and a pair has to fall together or the last one runs wild. Dog on each. Eggs broken early never hatch.",
    } },
    'Chrysalis': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She wrapped herself in silk at the hub, and it closes slowly. No blow to brace for, so I struck it with the rest of the line, everything we had, before it shut. It tore. She came out staggered and open.",
      gloomsworn: "She wrapped herself in silk at the hub, slowly. Nothing hit us, so everything we had went into the wrapping before it closed. It tore, and she tumbled out, dazed and open.",
      thresher: "She wrapped herself in silk like a shutter closing, slowly. No blow to dodge, so it was every scrap of damage we had into the silk before it shut. It tore, and out she tumbled, dazed and open. Quite undone, bless her.",
      bloodreaver: "She hid in a cocoon! Coward. I tore at it with every wound I had saved up, all of us did, before it closed. She tumbled out dazed and soft. Then the real bleeding started.",
      runeguard: "She binds herself in silk at the hub. Bring the whole party's force upon it before it closes; torn open, she staggers and lies exposed. Let it close, and she devours us all. For once the lesson is brevity.",
      gorgemaw: "She wrapped herself up like a parcel left for later. We tore into the wrapping with everything before it sealed, and she fell out stunned and tender. Best course of the night.",
      oathbroker: "She seals herself in silk at the hub; the debt falls due when it closes, and the penalty is everything. We paid in full beforehand, tearing it open. Torn, she staggers and pays heavily.",
      ironpalm: "She wrapped herself in silk and fell silent. Silence is a question too. We answered with everything we had, all at once. It tore.",
      ranger: "Cocooned at the hub. Every arrow into it before it closed. It tore. She fell out staggered, wide open.",
      shadowblade: "She sealed herself in silk at the hub. Hiding is my trade, darling, and she was doing it badly. We tore it open with everything before it closed; she came out staggered and soft.",
      pyromancer: "She wrapped herself in silk, near me, which is frankly an invitation! Everything I had, all of us, before it closed. It burst, and she fell out staggered. A very satisfying pop.",
      thermist: "Cocoon at the hub; it closes; tear it first. Ran scalding, poured all of it in, the silk blackening. It tore. She staggered, and I poured in more.",
      driftblade: "She wrapped herself in silk at the hub and for once I stood still and struck, everything I had, every blade we owned, and it tore and she tumbled out dazed.",
      marionettist: "She retires behind silk for a costume change, and the company storms the dressing room with everything before the door shuts. (She emerges staggered and exposed. Applause.)",
      cleric: "She sealed herself in silk at the hub, and venom dripped on us as it closed. I kept everyone on their feet so they could give it everything. It tore in time, and she fell out staggered.",
      grovekeeper: "She wrapped herself at the hub like a seed in its husk, waiting for her season. We tore the husk open with everything before it ripened. She fell out staggered. A good reaping.",
      chronomancer: "She wraps herself at the hub, and it closes on a slow count. Everything into it before the last beat. Torn, she staggers. Let it close and nobody is undone, not even by me.",
      alembist: "She cocoons at the hub; once closed, it is fatal. Every hand on it before it closes, the drips mended on the side. Note: torn open, she staggers and every blow bites deeper.",
      threadweaver: "She stitched herself into a cocoon at the hub, neat work for once. We unpicked it with everything we had before the last seam closed. She fell out staggered.",
      tidecaller: "She wrapped herself in silk at the hub, slowly, and only a little venom came at us while it closed. I kept the party on their feet and the water high, and everyone gave it all they had before it shut. It tore, and she fell out dazed.",
      lanternkeeper: "At the hub she began to wind herself shut, slowly, and a little venom fell while the silk closed. I held every lantern bright and kept us on our feet so the others could spend all they had. Before it sealed it tore, and she tumbled out dazed.",
      kenseier: "She began to wrap herself in silk at the hub, and she was slow about it. No blow came for me to meet, so I struck the cocoon with the rest, all we had, before it shut. It tore, and she fell out dazed.",
      houndmaster: "She started wrapping herself in silk at the hub, slow about it. Everything I had went into the cocoon before it closed. It tore, and she fell out staggered. Sent both dogs in.",
    } },
    'The Web Gives Way': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The floor fell ring by ring from the walls, each ring shuddering first. We moved in: the hub, the spokes, or silk we never cut. The last ring took the hub too, leaving spokes and the pit. A reel stops at the brink and holds taut; dash along the silk and it snaps.",
      gloomsworn: "The web dropped away in rings, each trembling first. The last took the hub as well, and the watch stood on spokes and the final silk with the pit about us. A reel stops dead at the brink, taut; dash along the silk to snap it.",
      thresher: "The floor went ring by ring, each shuddering first like a bank before it slips. I moved in on the shudder; the last ring took the hub too, leaving a few spokes and the pit round. A reel stops at the brink and holds, dear; dash the silk to snap it.",
      bloodreaver: "The floor fell ring by ring! Then the last took the hub, and it was the spokes and a pit! I fell once: hurt, held, hauled back up. Worth the bruise. She reeled me, and the thread stopped at the brink, so I dashed along the silk and snapped it.",
      runeguard: "A ring shudders before it falls; step inward onto the hub, a spoke, or a thread left uncut. A fourth ring takes the hub itself, leaving only the spokes and last silk, the pit about us, and she still lunges. A reel halts at the brink and holds; dash the silk to snap it.",
      gorgemaw: "The tablecloth pulled out from under us, ring by ring, even the centre in the end. We kept to the spokes and the last silk, a pit around the plate, while she still lunged and reeled across it. A reel stops at the table's edge, taut. Dash along the silk.",
      oathbroker: "The floor is repossessed ring by ring from the walls, each served notice by a shudder. The fourth takes the hub, leaving the spokes and last silk, the pit about us, and she still lunges. A reel stops at the brink and holds; dashing the silk snaps it.",
      ironpalm: "The floor trembled, then fell, ring by ring, and finally the hub. We stood on the spokes, the pit round us, and she still came. She reeled me, and the thread stopped at the brink, taut. I ran down the silk and it snapped.",
      ranger: "Ring shakes, then drops. From the walls in. Last ring takes the hub: just spokes and the last silk, pit all round. She still lunges, reels, drops. Reeled: stops at the brink, holds. Dash along the silk, it snaps. Narrow footing. Still a line.",
      shadowblade: "The floor shudders, then falls away, ring by ring. Inward, to the spokes, and finally the last ring took the hub, leaving nothing but silk and the pit. She still lunges and reels across it. A reel stops at the brink and holds. Dash the silk and it snaps.",
      pyromancer: "The floor started dropping away, ring by ring! I hate moving, but I hate falling more, so at each shudder I swept inward. The last ring took the hub too! Spokes, silk, pit all round. Reeled? It stops at the brink and holds. Dash along the silk, then keep casting.",
      thermist: "A ring shudders; it falls; the hub goes last, leaving spokes and silk with the pit round. She still lunges. I fell once, held, hauled up. Cold: stay on the silk. Reeled? It stops at the brink, taut. Hot: dash along it.",
      driftblade: "The floor fell away ring by ring, and in the end the hub went too, so I ran the spokes and the last silk with the pit all round, and when she reeled me the thread stopped at the brink, so I dashed along the silk and through, on what would last.",
      marionettist: "The stage collapses ring by ring, each trembling first. In the last act even the hub drops, leaving the company on the spokes and last silk, the pit about them. (Step off and be hauled up in disgrace.) Reeled? The thread stops at the brink; dash along the silk to snap it.",
      cleric: "The floor fell ring by ring, each shuddering first. In the end the hub itself went, and I kept our people on the spokes and the last silk. Those who stepped off I mended. A reel stopped at the brink and held; they dashed along the silk and it snapped.",
      grovekeeper: "The ground went from the walls inward like a riverbank in flood. The last slip took the hub itself, leaving my lot on the spokes and the silk with a pit round them. A reel stops at the brink and holds, dear; run the silk and it snaps. The uncut threads were bridges.",
      chronomancer: "The floor shakes, then falls, ring by ring. Step inside on the shake. The fourth ring takes the hub; only the spokes and last silk remain. When she reels, it stops at the brink and holds; dash along the silk. I was there already, it seems.",
      alembist: "The floor falls ring by ring after a shudder. The last takes the hub: spokes, last silk, a pit about us, and she still lunges. Note: those who fall are hurt and hauled up; keep the mending ready. Her reel stops at the brink and holds. Dash along the silk and it snaps.",
      threadweaver: "The floor fell ring by ring. The last took the hub itself, so I kept my stitches pinned to the spokes and the final silk, the pit all round. A reel stopped at the brink, taut; they dashed along the thread and it snapped. Her own silk, holding us up.",
      tidecaller: "The floor went ring by ring from the walls, each shuddering first. At the fourth the hub itself went, leaving the spokes and last silk with the pit round us. A tide takes a beach to the last rock. When she reeled us, it stopped at the brink; we ran the silk and it snapped.",
      lanternkeeper: "Each ring shuddered, then went, from the walls inward, and the last ring took the hub. I set my lanterns along the spokes and the last silk, the pit dark round us. Whoever fell came back up hurt. A reel stopped at the brink and held; they dashed down the silk toward the light.",
      kenseier: "The web fell in rings, each trembling first. The last took the hub, leaving the spokes and last silk with the pit round us, and she still lunged and dropped across it. The tremble is the beat. A reel halts at the brink and holds taut. Dash along the silk.",
      houndmaster: "Whole web's coming down, a ring at a time, each shivering first. Last one takes the hub: spokes and a bit of silk, pit all round, dogs close. She still lunges. Step off and you're hauled up hurt. Reeled, it stops at the brink; dash along the silk.",
    } },
    'Snare Weave': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Snares over every inch, woven twice from the hub, and three clear pockets each time. I took the one beside her, then the next round. Egg-bearers dropped theirs where they stood. Cramped, but a pocket is a post.",
      gloomsworn: "Snares across the floor from her hub, twice over, three pockets left clear each time. I stood in the one beside her, then in the next. The two who carried eggs set them down where they happened to be. Close quarters. I have lain in smaller.",
      thresher: "She laid snares over every inch of the cave, twice, leaving three bare patches, then three more a turn round her. I went patch to patch with the others, a hop apiece. The two carrying eggs set them down in the patch just left, never the one ahead.",
      bloodreaver: "Snares everywhere, twice over! Only three holes were clear, so I kept my friend company in the one by her and stepped round with her. The egg-carriers dropped their mess in the hole they had left. No scar at all.",
      runeguard: "She takes the hub and the web fills with snares, the second layer showing early, its pockets a turn round. Pocket, then the next one round; the egg-bearers drop theirs in the pocket just left, clear of the pillars. A diagram, in the end.",
      gorgemaw: "The table laid edge to edge with snares, two courses, leaving three clean plates bare. We moved plate to plate round her. The two given eggs set them down on the plate just finished. Well cleared, at least.",
      oathbroker: "Terms: the floor is forfeit but for three clear pockets, twice. We moved from one to the next round her. The rider: those carrying eggs must drop them in the pocket they leave, never the one ahead or behind a pillar.",
      ironpalm: "She filled the web with snares. Three quiet places. Then three more, a step round. We moved together. Those with eggs left them where they had stood.",
      ranger: "Snares on every inch but three holes, then three more a turn round. Took the far hole, then the next. Had the eggs once: waited, dropped it where I stood, moved on.",
      shadowblade: "Snares over everything, three bare pockets, then three more a turn along. I slipped from one to the next behind her. Given eggs, I left them in the pocket I was leaving, as one does.",
      pyromancer: "Snares everywhere, twice, and only three holes to stand in. I cast on from mine, then hopped round to the next. They hung eggs on me, so I dropped the lot where I had been, and did not look back.",
      thermist: "Three clear pockets; then three more, a turn round her. Step. Eggs on my back, the second layer coming. Dropped them where I stood, ran to the next pocket. Cooled off after.",
      driftblade: "Snares from wall to wall and only three holes. I went hole to hole round her, through the gap the first layer left, and dropped the eggs on me where I had been, already gone.",
      marionettist: "The whole stage strung with snares, leaving three bare marks, then three more a step round. The company moves mark to mark. The two carrying eggs leave them on the mark just struck. (The Doll took the inner mark.)",
      cleric: "She filled the web with snares and left only three clear pockets, then three more a little round. We went from one to the next. The two she laid eggs on left them where they had stood, out of everyone's way. Slow work; the faithful learn by doing.",
      grovekeeper: "She sowed the whole field with snares, leaving three clearings, then three more a turn on. My lot moved clearing to clearing. Those carrying eggs left them in the clearing behind, not the one ahead, dear.",
      chronomancer: "Two layers of snares, a few beats apart; the second's pockets show early, a turn round. I stepped round on the beat. The egg-carriers drop theirs in the pocket just left. I did, this time.",
      alembist: "Snares over the whole web, twice, with three clear pockets each. Pocket, then the next one round her. Note: the egg pools go in the pocket just left, never ahead or behind a pillar. Spoiled floor, labelled.",
      threadweaver: "Snares stitched over every inch, then a second layer, three holes each. I walked my stitches round from one to the next, and the two with eggs dropped them in the hole they had left. Buttons into buttonholes.",
      tidecaller: "She laid snares over the whole web, twice over, and left only three clear pockets each time, one by her for the knight and two far out. We went pocket to pocket as she turned. The two with eggs let them fall in the pocket just left.",
      lanternkeeper: "Snares covered the whole web, twice, and each time only three pockets stayed clear: one by her for the knight, two far out. We followed her turning from pocket to pocket, and the pair with eggs left them in the pocket just quit. I lit each new one.",
      kenseier: "Snares lay across the floor from her hub, woven twice, with three clear pockets each time. I took the one beside her, then the next. Those carrying eggs set them down where they stood. Cramped, and quiet, which suits me.",
      houndmaster: "Snares on every inch but three holes, then three more a turn on. Took the near hole, then the next, dogs in my tracks. Egg-bearers set the egg down where they stood.",
    } },
    'Scale Wave': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "She flew to the hub and beat her wings, and the dust rolled out in two waves. The first struck everything beyond the hub, so I kept the line in close under her; the second struck the hub itself, so we stepped out together as the first landed. In, then out.",
      gloomsworn: "She settled over the hub and beat her wings, and the dust rolled out twice. We stood close under her while the first wave struck the far ground, then walked out of the hub as it landed. Dust is dust, even to the dead. I stepped out anyway.",
      thresher: "She came to the hub and beat her wings, and the dust rolled out in two waves. We tucked in close under her while the first swept the far floor, then stepped out of the middle as it landed. In, then out, a tidy little dance.",
      bloodreaver: "She beat her wings over the hub and the dust came out in two waves! The first hit everything far out, so I stayed close under her and felt nothing; the second hit the hub, so I leapt clear as it came. I took the second once. Once was plenty.",
      runeguard: "She flew to the hub and her wings rose, and the order is written plainly: stay close beneath her while the first wave crosses the far floor, then step out of the hub the instant it lands, for the second falls upon the hub itself. In, then out.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess beat her wings over the table and the dust came as two courses. The first was served to the far end of the room, so I stayed close under her; the second was served at the centre, so I rose from it as the first landed. Overcooked.",
      oathbroker: "The terms: she flew to the hub and beat her wings, and the dust fell due in two instalments. The first is owed beyond the hub, so the company stood close under her; the second is owed by the hub, so we left it as the first came due.",
      ironpalm: "She beat her wings over the hub, and the dust spoke twice. The first word was for the far floor, so we stood close beneath her. The second was for the hub, so we stepped out as the first ended. In, then out.",
      ranger: "It flew to the middle and beat its wings. Dust in two rolls: far ground first, then the middle. Stayed tight under it for the first, left the middle the moment the first landed. In, then out.",
      shadowblade: "The mark settled over the hub and beat its wings, and the dust rolled out twice. I stayed close in under it, where the first wave could not reach me, then slipped out of the hub just as it landed. In, then out.",
      pyromancer: "She flew to the hub and beat her wings, and the dust rolled out in two waves, which ruined my spell twice! I stayed in close for the first, then bolted from the middle as it landed and finished casting from just outside. Spell finished, but very powdery.",
      thermist: "Wings over the hub, and the dust came in two waves. Stay in close for the first; the far floor takes it. Then the hub flushed red and I ran out, hot with hurry, before the second came down. In, then out.",
      driftblade: "The moth flew to the hub and beat her wings and the dust came rolling out twice, so I stayed in close under her while the first went past over the far floor and then ran out through the hub's edge as it landed and away before the second came down.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady takes centre stage and beats her wings, and the dust rolls out in two sweeps. The company tucks in close beneath her for the first, then exits the middle as it lands. (In, then out. Exit the Doll, upstage, powdered.)",
      cleric: "She came to the hub and beat her wings, and the dust rolled out in two waves. I gathered everyone in close beneath her for the first, then led them out of the middle as it landed, for the second fell on the hub itself.",
      grovekeeper: "She beat her wings over the hub and the dust came out in two waves, like pollen on a hard wind, dear. The far ground took the first, so my lot stayed in close under her; the second settled on the middle, so out we went as the first landed. In, then out.",
      chronomancer: "She beats her wings, and the dust comes twice: the far floor first, then the middle, a beat behind. I will have stayed in close for the first and stepped out of the hub as it landed. In, then out; the second was always going to be the middle.",
      alembist: "Powdered wing-dust, delivered in two doses: stay in close beneath her for the first, which falls on the far floor, then leave the hub the moment it lands, for the second falls on the hub itself. One sneeze, entirely avoidable.",
      threadweaver: "She beat her wings over the hub and the dust rolled out in two waves. We stayed in close, tied up under her for the first, then I drew my stitches out of the middle as it landed, before the second fell. No fuss: in, then out.",
      tidecaller: "She beat her wings over the hub, and the dust went out in two long swells, the far floor first and the middle after. We stayed close under her while the first rolled past, then walked out of the hub as it broke. There was no hurry, only an order.",
      lanternkeeper: "She came down to the hub and beat her wings, and the dust rolled out twice. I hung the lamps close under her for the first wave, which struck the far floor, then picked them up and set them just outside the hub as it landed. In, then out.",
      kenseier: "She beat her wings over the hub, and the dust came in two strokes. The first struck the far floor, so we stood close under her; the second struck the hub on the beat after, so we stepped out as the first landed. In, then out.",
      houndmaster: "She flew to the middle and beat her wings, and the dust came in two rolls. Dogs tight in under her for the first, far floor took it, then whistled them out of the middle as it landed. In, then out.",
    } },
    'Flutter': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "She flew three runs across the cave, each down a lane drawn through one of us: two of the others, then me. The lane follows its mark until the last moment, so the marked one steps clear as it sets, and the rest keep off the line.",
      gloomsworn: "Three passes across the cave, each down a strip drawn through one of us: two of the watch, then me. The strip follows its mark until the last moment, then stays. I stepped out of mine late. The rest kept clear of the line.",
      thresher: "Three passes across the cave, each down a lane through one of us: two of the others, then our knight. The lane follows its guest until just before she goes, so you sidestep at the end and she sails past. Each new lane shows only once she has landed.",
      bloodreaver: "Three runs across the cave, each down a lane through one of us, the others first and then me! The lane sticks to its mark until just before she flies, so I waited, then stepped out of the line. I stood in one once. A lesson, and a bruise.",
      runeguard: "A lane is drawn from her through one of us, following that one until the last moment. Two passes go to the others, the third to our shield. Sidestep as it sets, and keep clear of every lane not your own.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess flew three runs across the room, each down a lane drawn through one guest: two of the others, then me. The lane follows its guest until just before she goes, so I stepped aside at the last and let the dish go by. Thin fare.",
      oathbroker: "Three passes across the cave, each down a lane drawn through one party; two of the others, then myself. The lane tracks its subject until just before she flies, so the subject steps aside at the last, and the others keep clear.",
      ironpalm: "She crossed the cave three times, each pass down a lane through one of us. The lane followed its mark, then held still. I waited for it to hold, and stepped aside. The others stayed out of the line. Three questions, three answers.",
      ranger: "Three passes down the cave. Each along a line from it through one of us: two others, then the shield. Line follows the mark until the last moment, then sets. Step off it as it sets. Everyone else stays clear. Next line only drawn once it lands.",
      shadowblade: "Three passes down the cave, each along a lane through one of us: two of the others, then our knight. The lane follows its mark until the last moment, so I waited for it to set and stepped aside. She flew through where I had been.",
      pyromancer: "She flew three runs across the cave, each lane drawn right through whoever she picked! It follows you until the last moment, so I held my spell, then stepped out of the line at the last. The others stayed off it.",
      thermist: "Three passes; each lane runs from her through one of us. Others first, then the knight. It follows you until the last instant, then it sets. Step out of it then, sideways, quick. The rest kept off the line. The next is drawn only after she lands.",
      driftblade: "She flew three runs across the cave, each down a lane drawn through one of us, and mine followed me round until the last breath so I ran with it and then slipped out sideways when it set, and she shot past where I had been, and I was already round.",
      marionettist: "Three entrances across the cave, each along a lane drawn through one of the company: two of us, then our knight. The lane follows its player until just before the cue, then sets; step out on cue, and the others stay out of the wings. (Rehearse accordingly.)",
      cleric: "She flew three times across the cave, each down a lane drawn through one of us, the others first and our knight last. The lane follows its mark until just before she goes, so I stepped aside at the end and told the others to keep clear.",
      grovekeeper: "Three gusts straight across the cave, dear, each down a lane drawn through one of my lot: two of them, then our knight. The lane follows its sapling until the last moment, then sets, so I stepped aside only at the end. Read each in its turn.",
      chronomancer: "Three passes, each down a lane through one of us; each new lane appears only after the last pass ends. The lane follows its mark until just before she goes, so I stepped aside on the last beat, which I had already done. The knight gets the third, naturally.",
      alembist: "Three runs across the cave, each down a lane drawn through one of us: two of the others, then the knight. Note: the lane follows its mark until just before she flies, so the marked one sidesteps at the last and the rest stay off the line.",
      threadweaver: "Three runs across the cave, each along a lane drawn through one of my stitches: two of them, then the knight. The lane follows its stitch until just before she flies, so I stepped aside at the last and kept everyone clear of the line.",
      tidecaller: "She crossed the cave three times, each along a lane drawn through one of us: two of the others, then our knight. The lane follows its mark until just before she goes; I stepped aside as it settled and let the rush go by.",
      lanternkeeper: "Three passes across the cave, each down a lane through one of us: two of the others, then our knight. The lane follows its mark until just before she goes, so the marked one stepped aside at the last, and I kept the lamps off the line.",
      kenseier: "Three passes across the cave, each down a lane drawn through one of us, the others first, then me. The lane follows its mark until just before she flies; I stepped aside on the beat it set. The others kept off the line.",
      houndmaster: "Three runs across the cave, each down a lane through one of us: two of the others, then the shield-man. Lane sticks to you till just before she goes, then sets. Stepped off it sideways, dogs with me, and kept clear of the others' lines.",
    } },
    'Moonlight': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The silk rooted me where I stood, and a pillar of pale light fell on one of the others. The whole line has to share it, so the marked one came to me and everyone crowded in. We quit the dust it left once I could move.",
      gloomsworn: "The silk rooted me in place and a pillar of pale light came down on one of the watch. All of us must share it, so the marked one walked to me and the rest crowded in. The dust it left we quit as soon as I could walk.",
      thresher: "The silk pinned our knight, then a pillar of pale light picked one of the others, and the whole party must stand in it together. So the marked one came over and we all crowded in. The dust it leaves stings, dear, so step out of it after.",
      bloodreaver: "The silk pinned me, and a pillar of pale light picked one of the soft ones! All of us have to stand in it, so whoever it picked ran to me and the rest piled in. Shared, it barely stung. The dust it left behind bites, so I stayed out of that. Mostly.",
      runeguard: "The silk pins our knight, and a pillar of pale light settles on one of the others. The marked one goes to the knight and the whole party stands in the light together, for it is shared. The dust it leaves must be abandoned at once.",
      gorgemaw: "She pinned me to the spot, then a pillar of pale light fell upon one of my friends: a dish for the whole party. The marked one carried it over to me and we all took our share. The crumbs it left on the floor burn, so I moved off them. Bland, but filling.",
      oathbroker: "The terms: the silk holds me where I stand, and a pillar of pale light, owed by the whole party, names one of the others. The named one comes to me and the whole company stands in it. The dust it leaves is a charge on the floor; vacate it.",
      ironpalm: "The silk held me still. A pillar of pale light fell on one of the others, and it wanted all of us. The marked one came to me and we stood in it together, and it passed. The dust it left stayed behind. I walked away from it.",
      ranger: "Silk pinned the shield. Pale light picked one of the rest, shared by all of us. Marked one walked to the shield and we all closed in. No gap, just closing. Dust left on the floor after: stepped clear.",
      shadowblade: "The silk pinned our knight, and a pillar of pale light picked one of the rest of us, to be shared by all. The marked one went over to the knight and we crowded in, which I find undignified. The dust left behind stings, so I did not linger.",
      pyromancer: "Our knight was pinned, and a pillar of pale light came down on one of the others, to be shared by all of us! The marked one dashed to the knight and we crowded in, still casting. The dust it left stung my eyes, so out we went.",
      thermist: "The silk holds our knight, and a pale pillar picks one of the rest. It is shared: everyone in. The marked one ran to the knight, hot with hurry, and the rest crowded in. The dust left on the floor bites, so we left it, quickly.",
      driftblade: "The silk pinned our knight and the pale light picked one of us, and it had to be shared, which meant standing still with everyone, so I ran to the knight and crowded in and was out again the moment it had fallen, away from the dust it left behind.",
      marionettist: "Our knight is pinned centre stage, and a pillar of pale light picks one of the company: a scene for all of us. The marked one goes to the knight and the whole company crowds into the light. (The dust it leaves is no place to linger; the company exits.)",
      cleric: "Our knight was pinned by the silk, and a pillar of pale light fell upon one of the others. It is shared, so the marked one went to the knight and everyone gathered close. I mended what it cost, and we left the dust on the floor.",
      grovekeeper: "The silk rooted our knight, and a pillar of pale light came down on one of my lot, dear, for all of us to share. The marked one walked to the knight and the rest gathered round like a hedge. The dust left behind is no place to stand.",
      chronomancer: "The silk pins our knight, and then the pale light picks one of the others, and all of us stand in it together. The marked one walked to the knight and the rest closed in, as I recall doing. The dust lingers, so we left it the moment it fell.",
      alembist: "A pillar of pale light on one of the others, and it wants all of us. The marked one goes to our pinned knight and everyone crowds in together. Note: the dust it leaves on the floor is a nuisance and a hazard; leave it. Labelled.",
      threadweaver: "The silk pinned our knight, and a pillar of pale light picked one of my stitches. All of us share it, so the marked one came to the knight and the rest drew in on the thread. The dust it leaves is a snag; we stepped out of it quickly.",
      tidecaller: "Our knight was pinned, and a pillar of pale light settled on one of the others, to be shared by all. The marked one came to the knight and the rest drew in around them, like water filling a hollow. We left the dust to settle.",
      lanternkeeper: "The silk held our knight, and a pillar of pale light chose one of the others. It is shared, so the marked one carried their light over to the knight and the rest gathered in. Four of us, one pillar. The dust it leaves burns, so we left it behind.",
      kenseier: "The silk pinned me where I stood and a pillar of pale light came down on one of the others. It had to be shared by all, so the marked one came to me and we stood in it together. The dust it left was no place to stay.",
      houndmaster: "Silk pinned the shield-man, and a pillar of pale light picked one of the rest. Shared, so the marked one came to him and we all crowded in. Dust left on the floor after: whistled the dogs out of it.",
    } },
  },
};
